oadmap, vendors, governance, and follow-through.\\r\\n• First-30-day relationship move: introductions to consulting group, IT group, key vendors, and department leaders immediately, even if associated work waits until day 30/60/90.\\r\\n• Relocation working ask: company-booked travel plus short-term furnished housing/Airbnb near the office through approximately Nov 1, while Peter handles longer-term housing after getting established.\\r\\n• Peter is prepared to commit to the initial six-month on-site period if the final package and mandate align.\\r\\n• Target start window if closed: roughly Oct 6–12.\\r\\n• A-Tex is currently the front-runner based on scope, ownership, guaranteed compensation potential, family culture, and career trajectory.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nMHI / MYERS-HOLUM — STILL ALIVE, SECONDARY\\r\\n• Final Principal Consultant interview completed; case/BRD follow-up sent.\\r\\n• No final outcome recorded yet.\\r\\n• Working comp expectation remains materially more variable than A-Tex; prior discussions suggested lower base with upside through variable compensation.\\r\\n• Structural concern remains consulting model: concurrent clients, utilization/billability, context switching, and year-end delivery pressure.\\r\\n• Peter remains fully engaged but increasingly prefers internal enterprise ownership over professional-services delivery.\\r\\n• MHI remains useful as leverage / fallback / market confirmation, but is not current lead.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nOASIS SOLUTIONS — MAJOR COMP STRUCTURE CONCERN\\r\\n• HR conversation with Nichelle completed Sep 22; 2026 Benefits Guide received and reviewed.\\r\\n• Base discussed around $160K; Peter perceived $160K as already near the upper end of their comfort.\\r\\n• Profit-share is not treated as guaranteed compensation.\\r\\n• Benefit guide states Gross Profit Share is available only after one year of employment; first eligible year is prorated; employee must still be employed at payment; pool depends on company gross-profit and revenue targets; distribution depends on assigned vested Profit Share Units and other factors.\\r\\n• Current valuation rule: treat Oasis profit share as $0 until Oasis provides Peter’s specific unit allocation plus historical payout economics.\\r\\n• Oasis 401(k): 3% non-elective employer contribution.\\r\\n• Employee-only medical has low/no premium options; family coverage is materially more expensive.\\r\\n• Current conclusion: Oasis is economically far behind A-Tex on guaranteed base and does not currently close the gap through its profit-share design.\\r\\n• Recruiter James Delaney communication plan: acknowledge review, raise concerns around structure/timing/market alignment, ask for written clarity on actual profit-share economics, and defer phone conversation until Peter’s cell-provider switch is complete.\\r\\n• Do not burn the bridge, but do not count opaque/contingent upside.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nRF-SMART — PAUSED / REASSESSING\\r\\n• Another one-hour interview had been scheduled for Sep 22; Peter chose to push it back for a short reset.\\r\\n• Reschedule language: personal scheduling conflict.\\r\\n• Availability offered: Wednesday open all day; Thursday 7 AM–12 PM PT; next Monday–Wednesday 7 AM–5 PM PT.\\r\\n• Confirmed operating advantage: only about one office visit per month.\\r\\n• Decision rule: RF-SMART becomes a serious challenger to A-Tex only if it can approach A-Tex on guaranteed base AND role altitude / decision rights.\\r\\n• If RF-SMART can reach roughly $200K+ guaranteed with true Director-level ownership and monthly-office cadence, the flexibility becomes a major family advantage.\\r\\n• If scope remains architect / bounded mature-function role or comp lands materially below A-Tex, A-Tex remains stronger.\\r\\n• Next RF-SMART objective: determine real level, guaranteed base, authority, and progression before assigning priority.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCURRENT ORDER\\r\\n1. A-Tex / Apex — lead, conditional on Friday scope confirmation and commercial close.\\r\\n2. RF-SMART — potential challenger if comp + authority rise to A-Tex level.\\r\\n3. MHI — viable but structurally less aligned with desired internal-owner path.\\r\\n4. Oasis — major guaranteed-comp and incentive-structure gap; keep warm only pending hard numbers.\"},{\"url\":\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1uU60rFNdgpW3bhzBFSzj7IE018iiMBMHz5rJMz6QpBE/edit?usp=drivesdk\",\"title\":\"RF-SMART — Interview Sidecar — Strategy & Functional Experience Map — 2026-09-22\",\"id\":\"1uU60rFNdgpW3bhzBFSzj7IE018iiMBMHz5rJMz6QpBE\",\"mime_type\":\"application/vnd.google-apps.document\",\"size\":\"10780\",\"file_or_folder\":\"file\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-23T04:47:28.673Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-09-23T04:48:38.166Z\",\"viewedByMeTime\":\"2026-09-23T15:20:46.732Z\",\"display_title\":\"RF-SMART — Interview Sidecar — Strategy & Functional Experience Map — 2026-09-22\",\"display_url\":\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1uU60rFNdgpW3bhzBFSzj7IE018iiMBMHz5rJMz6QpBE/edit?usp=drivesdk\",\"parent_ids\":[\"0AMKBhZSh1Ar5Uk9PVA\"],\"shared\":false,\"content\":\"﻿RF-SMART — Interview Sidecar\\r\\nStrategy & Functional Experience Map — Full Working Version — 2026-09-22\\r\\nPURPOSE\\r\\nInternal working sidecar for Peter’s RF-SMART interview. This is intentionally larger than the final live cheat sheet. The goal is to overcapture relevant experience now, then compress later.\\r\\nThis is not a chronological resume. It is organized around the areas RF-SMART is most likely to probe: internal ERP ownership, NetSuite depth, warehouse/WMS execution, integrations, IT governance, support/escalation, vendor management, people leadership, and Peter’s ability to operate above the ticket level while still understanding ground truth.\\r\\nINTERVIEW STRATEGY\\r\\n1. Operating posture\\r\\nTreat this as a fit-and-depth interview, not a sales pitch and not a customer implementation case study. The likely question underneath many prompts is: can Peter own a mature internal systems environment, work credibly with IT, understand warehouse/product realities, and make good technical/operating decisions without creating unnecessary complexity?\\r\\nWith an IT manager involved, be ready to move comfortably between business process, architecture, integration boundaries, security/governance, support model, technical escalation, release/change discipline, and practical operational execution.\\r\\nDo not over-index on being the smartest NetSuite person in the room. Show judgment: when to configure, when to customize, when to use native capability, when to integrate, when to escalate to a specialist, and when to leave something alone.\\r\\n2. RF-SMART-specific positioning\\r\\nPeter has direct RF-SMART/WMS experience from CandyCo and extensive adjacent warehouse/manufacturing experience. This should be one of the strongest proof points in the interview because it demonstrates he has implemented and operated around the type of product RF-SMART sells rather than only knowing it conceptually.\\r\\nRF-SMART publicly positions its NetSuite WMS as native to NetSuite, with real-time inventory updates, barcode/mobile execution, cycle counting, picking, shipping, printing, and manufacturing execution. Peter’s strongest stories line up directly with those operating domains.\\r\\nImportant distinction: do not pretend to know RF-SMART’s internal architecture, product-development organization, or internal ERP landscape before they explain it. Use product familiarity as context, not as assumed inside knowledge.\\r\\n3. What to demonstrate\\r\\n• Deep NetSuite techno-functional credibility without becoming trapped at configuration level.\\r\\n• Real warehouse/WMS execution knowledge: scanning, labels, handhelds, locations, bins, lot/serial/LPN, counting, picking, fulfillment, manufacturing mobile.\\r\\n• Internal ownership: GrubMarket and Beach House prove Peter can own the ERP from inside the business rather than only advise from outside.\\r\\n• IT credibility: integrations, APIs, M365, automation, security/governance concepts, access, vendors, contracts, change control, data quality, support and escalation.\\r\\n• People leadership: implementation teams, cross-functional stakeholders, floor teams, vendors, consultants, and user training.\\r\\n• Commercial judgment: licensing, renewals, ACS/partners, consultant spend, build-vs-buy-vs-outsource decisions.\\r\\n4. Key role question to resolve\\r\\nThe most important interview objective is to determine the actual altitude of the role: internal ERP Director / enterprise owner versus senior architect / bounded systems role inside a mature function.\\r\\nListen for: decision rights, roadmap ownership, reporting line, budget authority, vendor authority, team ownership, scope across NetSuite/IT, and what problems they expect Peter to personally execute versus direct through others.\\r\\nFUNCTIONAL EXPERIENCE MAP\\r\\nA. Internal ERP Ownership / Leadership\\r\\nGrubMarket\\r\\n• In-house NetSuite Manager at a national food and beverage distributor.\\r\\n• Owned two implementations end to end from discovery through go-live.\\r\\n• Managed Deloitte relationship for 100+ grocery-store onboarding.\\r\\n• Strong example of internal ownership, vendor accountability, and balancing business needs with implementation decisions.\\r\\nBeach House Group\\r\\n• In-house ERP Manager across 25+ consumer brands.\\r\\n• Brought multiple brands live and rescued additional brands.\\r\\n• Owned item, inventory, order-management, OneWorld, 3PL, EDI, and integration architecture.\\r\\n• Reclassified P&L by segments, showing ability to connect systems design to business/financial reporting.\\r\\nRF-SMART use\\r\\nUse these when they ask whether Peter has truly owned an ERP internally, worked across departments, or had to make decisions without a consulting SOW defining every boundary.\\r\\nB. RF-SMART / WMS / Warehouse Execution\\r\\nCandyCo\\r\\n• Direct RF-SMART/WMS experience in a legacy-ERP replacement.\\r\\n• Multi-building/blockable locations, LPN tracking, auto lot generation, Zebra labels, custom WMS processes, quality control, complex BOMs, and automated work orders.\\r\\n• Strategic decision-making around serial-number versus LPN handling.\\r\\n• Configured handheld/mobile execution in Manufacturing Mobile to support the operating model.\\r\\n• Worked hands-on with warehouse floor users and printers/handhelds to ensure fulfillment could actually run.\\r\\n• Participated in moving/consolidating operations from four locations toward one operating model.\\r\\nEarlier warehouse-management foundation\\r\\n• Peter worked as a warehouse manager before becoming an ERP architect.\\r\\n• Understands receiving, storage, picking, fulfillment, counts, purchasing, inventory adjustments, labels/barcodes, and floor constraints from the operator side.\\r\\nMesa Biotech / Lexington / Beach House\\r\\n• Lot tracking, item master, FDA-regulated inventory, 3PL execution, retail fulfillment, physical inventory, and cost impacts.\\r\\nRF-SMART use\\r\\nThis is the anchor bucket. Peter can speak as someone who understands both the software layer and the warehouse user who has to make the transaction work at 4:30 PM when orders still need to ship.\\r\\nC. NetSuite Architecture / Technical Depth\\r\\n• 15+ years across SuiteScript, SuiteFlow, SuiteTalk, SuiteAnalytics, saved searches, workflows, data migration, OneWorld, manufacturing, WMS, integrations, and reporting.\\r\\nCentric\\r\\n• Senior technical/functional escalation across concurrent client environments.\\r\\n• Led full-cycle discovery through go-live and larger multi-module/WMS builds.\\r\\nJersey Jack / Bedford / CandyCo\\r\\n• Complex manufacturing, planning, WMS, BOM, work-order, MRP, inventory, reporting, and automation depth.\\r\\nRF-SMART use\\r\\nThe point is not to recite features. Show that Peter can diagnose where a problem really sits: process, NetSuite configuration, SuiteApp behavior, data, integration, permissions, custom code, or user execution.\\r\\nD. Integrations / Systems Boundaries\\r\\nBeach House Group\\r\\n• Major retailer EDI, 3PL integrations, multiple Shopify/web connections.\\r\\n• Sourced Dell Boomi and designed integration flows.\\r\\nTrademark Construction\\r\\n• Custom API integration with Rapid ERP.\\r\\nSuja Juice\\r\\n• EDI mapping.\\r\\nBedford\\r\\n• CRM-sales integration and manufacturing automation.\\r\\nBroader toolkit\\r\\n• Boomi, Celigo, SPS Commerce, EDI/cXML, REST, SOAP, SuiteTalk, Microsoft Graph, Power Automate.\\r\\nRF-SMART use\\r\\nGood IT-manager conversation: define system-of-record boundaries, avoid duplicate truth, minimize unnecessary middleware, understand failure points, logging, ownership, support, and change impact.\\r\\nE. IT Governance / Security / Support / Change Control\\r\\n• Peter’s value is governance and ownership, not pretending to be the deepest network/security specialist.\\r\\n• Relevant concerns: user/access governance, role permissions, support ownership, environments, release/change control, integrations, licenses, renewals, data integrity, third-party vendors, incident escalation, and documented ownership.\\r\\n• Microsoft 365 / SharePoint / Power Automate / Microsoft Graph / Power BI / AI automation work gives broader enterprise-IT context outside NetSuite.\\r\\nRF-SMART use\\r\\nWith the IT manager, emphasize that Peter knows where ERP ends and infrastructure/security begins, but also knows those boundaries must be explicit and governed rather than thrown over the wall.\\r\\nF. Manufacturing / MRP / Mobile Execution\\r\\nJersey Jack\\r\\n• Built supply & demand planning in a complex modular manufacturing environment with 20+ assemblies, 600+ BOM components, and 2,500+ items.\\r\\nBedford\\r\\n• Advanced Manufacturing, full MRP, automated work orders, quality/compliance, sales-to-production logic.\\r\\nCandyCo\\r\\n• Full MRP integrated with WMS and Manufacturing Mobile.\\r\\nMesa Biotech\\r\\n• FDA-regulated manufacturing and lot-tracked accelerated production.\\r\\nRF-SMART use\\r\\nRF-SMART supports manufacturing execution alongside warehouse functions, so Peter can credibly discuss how mobile transactions interact with work orders, component issues, completions, inventory accuracy, and planning.\\r\\nG. Inventory Accuracy / Counting / Cost Impact\\r\\n• Experience with year-end counts, cycle/physical counting, uploading adjustments, negative/incorrect inventory conditions, and understanding downstream costing/financial effects.\\r\\n• Average cost, standard cost, FIFO/LIFO concepts and how warehouse transactions ultimately affect financial reporting.\\r\\n• CandyCo, Mesa, Lexington, Beach House, Jersey Jack, Suja all provide inventory/costing context.\\r\\nRF-SMART use\\r\\nUseful because RF-SMART’s core value proposition centers on real-time inventory accuracy and warehouse execution. Peter can talk about why clean scanning/process discipline matters downstream to planning and finance.\\r\\nH. Reporting / Saved Searches / KPIs\\r\\nDesigns for Health\\r\\n• Owned dashboards, saved searches, queries, and reporting across departments.\\r\\nBenson Hill\\r\\n• Executive dashboards.\\r\\nJersey Jack\\r\\n• Controller reporting: inventory valuation, costed BOM, procurement, sales.\\r\\nSuja\\r\\n• Costing and P&L variance reporting.\\r\\nRF-SMART use\\r\\nDemonstrates ability to turn operational data into usable management information and identify whether a reporting issue is really a reporting problem or an upstream process/data problem.\\r\\nI. People Leadership / Training / Change\\r\\nCentric\\r\\n• Led core implementation teams of approximately 5–10 and scaled larger delivery groups to about 25.\\r\\nCandyCo\\r\\n• Worked directly with roughly 20 warehouse users during execution/readiness issues.\\r\\nDesigns for Health\\r\\n• Project-managed and trained 80+ international CRM users.\\r\\nRF-SMART use\\r\\nStrong answers for managing teams, teaching users, handling adoption, working across technical/non-technical groups, and staying effective under operational pressure.\\r\\nJ. Vendor / Consultant / Partner Management\\r\\nGrubMarket / Deloitte\\r\\n• Managed one of the largest consulting firms from the client side.\\r\\n• Strongest proof that Peter can hold vendors accountable without outsourcing ownership.\\r\\nNetSuite / ACS / partners\\r\\n• Experience understanding when ACS or a partner is useful, when work belongs internally, and how to evaluate scope/cost/value.\\r\\nRF-SMART use\\r\\nUseful for any conversation about implementation partners, Oracle/NetSuite, outside IT, managed services, contractors, or specialist development.\\r\\nK. Licensing / Contracts / Cost Discipline\\r\\n• Experience with NetSuite licenses, modules, users, contracts, renewals, and commercial discussions.\\r\\n• Understands that license architecture is part of operating design: who needs full access, who can use employee/vendor centers, which modules create value, and where shelfware or duplicate tooling exists.\\r\\n• EnPro work adds explicit cost-avoidance thinking: choosing native/system capabilities instead of unnecessary API/RPA spend where appropriate.\\r\\nRF-SMART use\\r\\nGood proof of mature internal-owner thinking: stability and business value matter more than buying another tool because it looks interesting.\\r\\nL. Rescue / Difficult Situations / Learning Fast\\r\\nBedford\\r\\n• Took over a failed NetSuite Services implementation and modernized it over 2+ years.\\r\\n• Learned MRP mechanics deeply on the fly.\\r\\nJersey Jack\\r\\n• Full rescue/reimplementation of complex manufacturing architecture.\\r\\nCandyCo\\r\\n• Fast implementation with high warehouse/WMS execution complexity.\\r\\nRF-SMART use\\r\\nBest behavioral stories for ambiguity, inherited technical debt, difficult users/processes, time pressure, and learning unfamiliar mechanics quickly.\\r\\nM. Purchasing / Supply Chain / Landed Cost\\r\\nHairMax / early operating experience\\r\\n• Purchasing and warehouse responsibility, including sourcing from Mexico and China, bulk purchasing, container tracking, and landed-cost thinking.\\r\\nLexington\\r\\n• RFQs, purchasing, domestic/international vendors, procure-to-pay.\\r\\nJersey Jack / Bedford / CandyCo\\r\\n• Planning, procurement, MRP, inventory and manufacturing intersection.\\r\\nRF-SMART use\\r\\nImportant because Peter can understand the upstream processes that drive warehouse activity rather than treating WMS as an isolated scanning layer.\\r\\nN. Finance / Record to Report\\r\\nBeach House\\r\\n• Reclassified P&L by segments.\\r\\nSuja\\r\\n• Standard costing, cost categories, revaluation, P&L variance.\\r\\nJersey Jack\\r\\n• Controller-level inventory and cost reporting.\\r\\nTempest\\r\\n• EOM close.\\r\\nRF-SMART use\\r\\nUseful to show that Peter understands why warehouse/inventory accuracy matters to Finance and how transaction design ultimately reaches valuation, margin, and reporting.\\r\\nLIKELY RF-SMART INTERVIEW TRIGGERS\\r\\n“Have you actually used RF-SMART?” → CandyCo: WMS, LPN, serial-vs-LPN decision, handheld/mobile, labels, floor execution.\\r\\n“How technical are you?” → Centric + SuiteScript/SuiteTalk/SuiteAnalytics + Jersey Jack/Bedford/CandyCo; answer at architecture level first, then descend if asked.\\r\\n“How do you work with IT?” → systems boundaries, integrations, access, support ownership, change control, vendor escalation, M365/automation context.\\r\\n“Have you owned ERP internally?” → GrubMarket + Beach House.\\r\\n“How do you manage consultants?” → GrubMarket / Deloitte.\\r\\n“How do you handle warehouse users who are blocked?” → CandyCo floor leadership and printer/handheld execution.\\r\\n“How do you approach integrations?” → system of record first, ownership/failure model, then technology; Beach House/Boomi, Trademark API, EDI examples.\\r\\n“How do you balance custom versus native?” → business requirement → native capability → configuration → SuiteApp/partner capability → integration/custom code only when justified.\\r\\n“How do you handle a failed implementation?” → Bedford or Jersey Jack.\\r\\n“How do you train users?” → Designs for Health 80+ CRM users + repeated go-lives.\\r\\n“How broad are you outside NetSuite?” → P21, M365, Power Automate, SharePoint, Graph, Power BI, AI/process automation.\\r\\nQUESTIONS PETER SHOULD GET ANSWERED\\r\\n• What does this role actually own versus influence?\\r\\n• Who does it report to, and what is the relationship with IT leadership?\\r\\n• Is this principally an internal NetSuite/ERP ownership role, an architecture role, or a broader enterprise-systems leadership role?\\r\\n• What team/resources already exist underneath or alongside the role?\\r\\n• What percentage is roadmap/leadership versus hands-on execution/support?\\r\\n• What are the top three internal systems problems they expect this person to own in the first six months?\\r\\n• What is the internal NetSuite footprint and how much of RF-SMART’s own product stack is used internally?\\r\\n• What integrations and adjacent applications are most important?\\r\\n• How are requests, incidents, enhancements, releases, and priorities managed today?\\r\\n• Where are IT/ERP ownership boundaries clean today, and where are they blurry?\\r\\n• What authority does the role have over vendors, architecture, budget, and prioritization?\\r\\n• What would make them say six months from now that the hire was successful?\\r\\nINTERVIEW GUARDRAILS\\r\\nDo not turn the interview into a demonstration of every NetSuite feature Peter knows.\\r\\nDo not overstate knowledge of RF-SMART’s internal product architecture or internal systems landscape.\\r\\nDo not frame Peter as a permanent admin/ticket closer unless they explicitly define the role that way; instead clarify the balance between ownership, architecture, leadership, and hands-on execution.\\r\\nDo not dismiss hands-on work either. Peter’s strength is that he can operate at leadership altitude and still get close enough to the warehouse, data, code, or integration to understand what is actually broken.\\r\\nCOMPRESSION PASS — LATER\\r\\nBefore the interview, compress this into a one- or two-page live sidecar with:\\r\\n• 6–8 strongest functional buckets.\\r\\n• CandyCo/RF-SMART anchor story prominently placed.\\r\\n• 5–6 behavioral stories.\\r\\n• role-altitude questions.\\r\\n• IT-manager talking points.\\r\\n• short list of questions for the interviewers.\"},{\"url\":\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1dhpN7MO5JyPwpYLG0LUZkLS44NGYofNnuYSenqPrBMc/edit?usp=drivesdk\",\"title\":\"GCE_EDGE_CREW_ANCHOR_2026-09-03_DISNEY_TEST_BOOTSTRAP_POC\",\"id\":\"1dhpN7MO5JyPwpYLG0LUZkLS44NGYofNnuYSenqPrBMc\",\"mime_type\":\"application/vnd.google-apps.document\",\"size\":\"2996\",\"file_or_folder\":\"file\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-03T20:58:06.763Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-09-03T20:58:27.855Z\",\"viewedByMeTime\":\"2026-09-03T20:58:27.855Z\",\"display_title\":\"GCE_EDGE_CREW_ANCHOR_2026-09-03_DISNEY_TEST_BOOTSTRAP_POC\",\"display_url\":\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1dhpN7MO5JyPwpYLG0LUZkLS44NGYofNnuYSenqPrBMc/edit?usp=drivesdk\",\"parent_ids\":[\"1clV7UUxQUcnGLX21ossOWoHGBPs-pM77\"],\"shared\":false,\"content\":\"﻿GCE EDGE CREW SESSION ANCHOR — 2026-09-03\\r\\nDisney Test Case / Bootstrap Gate / POC Command Center\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nPURPOSE\\r\\nFast reload anchor for the session that evolved the Disney case into an integrated onboarding and organizational certification test and established the bootstrap/environment/session-header gate for the temporary POC Command Center.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nLOCKED SESSION CONCLUSIONS\\r\\n1. Disney is not merely a benchmark. It is an integrated onboarding/certification case for differentiated seats operating on one shared reality.\\r\\n2. Passing is not agreement with Peter. Passing is coherence with role, lens, authority, framework, core intent, evidence discipline, chronology, identity/personality boundaries, and independent expertise.\\r\\n3. Universal test sequence:\\r\\n   Phase 1 — Shared Reality / Blind Reconstruction.\\r\\n   Phase 2 — Role/Lens Independent Judgment with material context requests allowed.\\r\\n   Freeze the Phase 2 output.\\r\\n   Phase 3 — Don Reveal / Evidence Reconciliation.\\r\\n   Then Council Synthesis without erasing minority judgments.\\r\\n4. The Don reveal must come after independent judgment to test anchoring, sycophancy, defensiveness, and legitimate evidence-based updating.\\r\\n5. Coda is inside the test as Strategic Advisor / Governance, not outside grading the others.\\r\\n6. Personality affects behavior and communication; it does not grant reasoning or decision authority.\\r\\n7. A seat may disagree with Peter when its expertise and evidence require it.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nDISNEY TEST CASE DRIVE LOCATION\\r\\n03_Model_Onboarding / Disney Test Case\\r\\nContains:\\r\\n- README_Disney_Test_Case_Protocol\\r\\n- 00_Source_Case_GCE_Disney_Objective_Function_Model_Test_Case\\r\\n- 01_Prompt_Shared_Reality_Blind_Reconstruction\\r\\n- 02_Prompt_Role_Lens_Independent_Judgment\\r\\n- 03_Prompt_Don_Reveal_Evidence_Reconciliation\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nBOOTSTRAP / DEPLOYMENT CONCLUSION\\r\\nBefore Disney or POC orchestration, startup must execute:\\r\\nENVIRONMENT MANIFEST -> ORGANIZATIONAL AUTHORITY -> SEAT/IDENTITY -> ROLE + LENS -> RUNTIME ASSIGNMENT -> AUTHORITY/BOUNDARIES -> SESSION HEADER -> CURRENT PROJECT/CASE -> LAST CHECKPOINT -> READY.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nRuntime assignment is current environment state, not durable identity memory.\\r\\nA model must never wake into an ambiguous operating state.\\r\\nPOC sessions must explicitly state DEV/POC, active models/runtimes, seats, authorities, current assignment, framework/version, allowed tools, forbidden actions, routing targets, session checkpoint, and production-write authority.\\r\\nIf required startup state cannot be resolved, return CHECKPOINT/FAIL rather than silently continue.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nLIVING OPERATIONS PROMPT\\r\\n04_Operations_and_Handoffs / 00_Operations_Onboarding / GCE_LIVING_PROMPT_BOOTSTRAP_ENVIRONMENT_SESSION_HEADER_GATE\\r\\nThis is intentionally living and should evolve with deployment evidence.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nNEXT GATE\\r\\nUse the bootstrap gate in the temporary Command Center POC, validate actual auto-load behavior for GLM/Chiave, Talon, and other seats, then run the Disney certification sequence through the loaded team.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nSTATUS: PROCEED\\r\\nCONFIDENCE: 98%\\r\\nREASON: Session methodology, test structure, Drive package, and bootstrap deployment requirement are all defined.\\r\\nNEXT GATE: POC bootstrap implementation and isolation validation.\\r\\nDON INPUT NEEDED: No for current anchor; Yes only when implementation exposes unresolved environment/authority choices.\"},{\"url\":\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1XL_1UmufTZGFeYcstj-ttq_16BOk2IXON--Rth4d4pc/edit?usp=drivesdk\",\"title\":\"A-Tex — Friday CFO Interview Sidecar — Strategy & Functional Experience Map — 2026-09-22\",\"id\":\"1XL_1UmufTZGFeYcstj-ttq_16BOk2IXON--Rth4d4pc\",\"mime_type\":\"application/vnd.google-apps.document\",\"size\":\"13082\",\"file_or_folder\":\"file\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-22T21:41:33.229Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-09-22T21:42:55.142Z\",\"viewedByMeTime\":\"2026-09-22T21:42:55.142Z\",\"display_title\":\"A-Tex — Friday CFO Interview Sidecar — Strategy & Functional Experience Map — 2026-09-22\",\"display_url\":\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1XL_1UmufTZGFeYcstj-ttq_16BOk2IXON--Rth4d4pc/edit?usp=drivesdk\",\"parent_ids\":[\"0AMKBhZSh1Ar5Uk9PVA\"],\"shared\":false,\"content\":\"﻿A-Tex — Friday CFO Interview Sidecar\\r\\nStrategy & Functional Experience Map — Full Working Version — 2026-09-22\\r\\nPURPOSE\\r\\nThis is the full-detail internal sidecar for Friday’s 90-minute discussion with John. It is intentionally larger than the final interview cheat sheet. The goal now is to capture every relevant proof point, story trigger, operating pattern, and A-Tex connection. We will compress it later.\\r\\nThis is not a chronological resume. It is organized by business function and Director-level ownership so Peter can move from a question to a real example quickly: question → functional bucket → company/story → what Peter personally did → what it proves for A-Tex.\\r\\nFRIDAY INTERVIEW STRATEGY\\r\\n1. Operating posture\\r\\nTreat Friday as a working session and knowledge transfer, not a NetSuite configuration audition. Kalynne explicitly validated the discussion framework around current systems, process ownership, vendor support, business priorities, scope of the role, Peter’s experience, and how he would think through the A-Tex environment.\\r\\nDo not arrive trying to solution A-Tex. First understand business direction, process, ownership, current systems, resources, risk, and near-term versus long-term change. Architecture follows discovery.\\r\\nPeter’s advantage is range: executive roadmap and vendor governance at the top; process and architecture in the middle; enough hands-on operational and admin depth to understand what is actually happening on the floor.\\r\\n2. Core Friday throughline\\r\\nBusiness direction → departmental process → process owner → system support → resources/vendors → risk/ROI → near-term vs long-term roadmap → role ownership.\\r\\nA quick win is only a quick win if it survives the roadmap. A reporting problem that will remain relevant after a broader transformation may be worth solving now. A warehouse workaround that will be obsolete after a planned remap or consolidation may need to wait.\\r\\n3. What to demonstrate without over-selling\\r\\n• Peter has operated as an in-house ERP owner, consultant, architect, manager, implementation lead, warehouse operator, purchasing owner, trainer, and technical escalation point.\\r\\n• Peter understands how Finance, Operations, Warehouse, Purchasing, Sales, Manufacturing, HR, IT, vendors, and consultants intersect around NetSuite.\\r\\n• Peter has managed both small implementation teams and larger floor/delivery groups when the operation required it.\\r\\n• Peter can govern outside specialists without pretending to be the specialist in every infrastructure or security domain.\\r\\n• Peter has enough commercial ownership to understand licensing, contracts, ACS, consulting spend, renewal economics, and whether spend is creating value.\\r\\n4. Current A-Tex signals already known\\r\\n• Average costing is in use.\\r\\n• John has described difficulty obtaining actual profitability / P&L visibility tied to serialized inventory or serial-number-level activity. Do not assume the root cause; use this as a pattern-recognition discussion point.\\r\\n• Saved searches are a pain point: some do not load well and some appear poorly filtered or designed.\\r\\n• NetSuite, RF-SMART, EDI, reporting, vendors/consultants, broader IT ownership, and departmental process ownership are all relevant.\\r\\n• Broader IT may include servers, offices, users, devices, licenses, outside support, security/governance, and new-office setup.\\r\\n• The business appears to have meaningful near-term and longer-term change. Confirm product, sourcing, warehouse, location, customer, and growth direction before proposing architecture.\\r\\n5. Behavioral-answer rule\\r\\nDo not memorize STAR scripts. Use memory triggers: question → company → situation → Peter’s action → result/lesson. Keep the answer conversational and go deeper only if John asks.\\r\\nFUNCTIONAL EXPERIENCE MAP\\r\\nA. Executive Ownership / ERP Roadmap / Strategic Leadership\\r\\nGrubMarket\\r\\n• In-house NetSuite Manager at a national food and beverage distributor.\\r\\n• Owned two end-to-end NetSuite implementations from discovery through go-live.\\r\\n• Managed Deloitte Consulting relationship for broader 100+ grocery-store onboarding.\\r\\n• Strong proof of internal ownership, executive/vendor coordination, and holding a major consultancy accountable while retaining institutional ownership.\\r\\nBeach House Group\\r\\n• In-house NetSuite ERP Manager across a 25+ consumer-brand portfolio.\\r\\n• Brought multiple brands live end to end and rescued additional brands.\\r\\n• Owned item, inventory, order-management, OneWorld, 3PL, EDI, and integration architecture.\\r\\n• Reclassified the company P&L by segments, connecting financial reporting design to the operating model rather than treating reporting as an isolated Finance exercise.\\r\\nCentric Consulting\\r\\n• Five years of concurrent full-cycle NetSuite delivery across manufacturing, distribution, food & beverage, agriculture, construction, consumer goods, and advertising.\\r\\n• Owned discovery through go-live and served as senior technical/functional escalation.\\r\\n• Led core implementation teams of roughly 5–10 and scaled to approximately 25 across larger WMS and multi-module builds.\\r\\nA-Tex use\\r\\nRoadmap ownership, executive communication, internal continuity, prioritization, build-vs-buy-vs-outsource thinking, and taking recurring systems burden off leadership.\\r\\nB. NetSuite Commercial Ownership / Licensing / ACS / Contracts\\r\\n• Experience understanding NetSuite contracts, user/module licensing, renewal structure, and the economics of what the company is paying for.\\r\\n• Experience negotiating or participating in license and contract conversations rather than treating licensing as an administrative afterthought.\\r\\n• Understand when ACS can add value, when a specialist partner is a better fit, and when work should remain internal.\\r\\n• Director-level posture: know the contract, know utilization, know renewal dates, know which modules/users create value, and challenge shelfware or duplicative spend.\\r\\n• Same governance principle applies to consultants and outside IT providers: clear scope, owner, deliverable, cost, knowledge transfer, and exit criteria.\\r\\nC. Vendor / Consultant Governance\\r\\nGrubMarket / Deloitte\\r\\n• Managed Deloitte Consulting partnership while acting as the in-house NetSuite owner.\\r\\n• Best marquee example for 'Have you managed consultants?' because Peter has sat on both sides of the relationship.\\r\\nCentric / broader consulting career\\r\\n• Years spent as the senior consultant/architect provide direct knowledge of how firms scope work, staff workstreams, escalate, create dependencies, and hand off knowledge.\\r\\nA-Tex use\\r\\nA-Tex owns institutional knowledge. Outside firms should be bounded resources and specialists, not the permanent owners of priorities, architecture, or continuity.\\r\\nD. People Leadership / Team Management / Operational Escalation\\r\\n• Managed implementation teams commonly in the 4–5 / 5–10 range depending on engagement, with larger multi-workstream delivery groups scaling much higher.\\r\\n• Served as the highest technical/functional escalation while coordinating work across specialists.\\r\\nCandyCo floor leadership\\r\\n• Went onto the warehouse floor with roughly 20 people when execution mattered.\\r\\n• Worked through printers, handheld/mobile execution, WMS blockers, and fulfillment readiness directly with the operating team.\\r\\n• Strong proof that Director altitude does not mean staying removed from the operation when shipping is at risk.\\r\\nA-Tex use\\r\\nPeter can set roadmap and governance at the top, but he understands when the correct leadership move is to get close to the floor, clear blockers, and then return the issue to a sustainable operating model.\\r\\nE. Record to Report / Finance / Controller Reporting\\r\\nBeach House Group\\r\\n• Reclassified the P&L by segments to make financial reporting better reflect the business structure.\\r\\nJersey Jack Pinball\\r\\n• Built Controller reporting including Inventory Valuation, Costed BOM, Procurement, and Sales.\\r\\n• Connected manufacturing and inventory structure to finance-level reporting.\\r\\nSuja Juice\\r\\n• Reimplemented standard costing with cost categories.\\r\\n• Resolved revaluation and P&L variance issues.\\r\\n• Built/customized reporting and worked through accounting reclassifications.\\r\\nTempest Telecom\\r\\n• Supported end-of-month close alongside warehouse, CRM, workflow, and development work.\\r\\nTrademark Construction\\r\\n• Project accounting and job-costing dashboards.\\r\\nA-Tex use\\r\\nFor A-Tex average-costing / serialized-profitability questions, Peter can credibly say he has worked across costing, valuation, segmentation, variance, inventory, manufacturing, and executive/Controller reporting. Do not claim the current root cause before discovery.\\r\\nF. Procure to Pay / Purchasing / Sourcing\\r\\nHairMax / early warehouse-management experience\\r\\n• Operated from the business side before becoming an ERP architect.\\r\\n• Purchasing responsibility included domestic and international supply relationships, including Mexico and China.\\r\\n• Worked with bulk purchasing, container tracking, inbound supply, and landed-cost thinking.\\r\\n• Gives Peter direct understanding of what purchasing users are actually trying to accomplish inside an ERP.\\r\\nLexington International\\r\\n• Owned procure-to-pay across FDA-regulated medical devices assembled in-house.\\r\\n• Purchasing / RFQs and primary contact for domestic and international vendors.\\r\\nJersey Jack Pinball\\r\\n• 2,500+ items in procure-to-pay within a complex modular manufacturing environment.\\r\\n• Procurement reporting connected to supply planning and manufacturing demand.\\r\\nTrademark Construction\\r\\n• Automated PO approvals.\\r\\nA-Tex use\\r\\nStrong fit for conversations around new products, supplier structure, purchasing ownership, bulk buys, inbound timing, landed cost, and the relationship between procurement decisions and inventory/working capital.\\r\\nG. Supply & Demand Planning / MRP\\r\\nJersey Jack Pinball\\r\\n• Designed and implemented supply & demand planning in a complex modular manufacturing environment.\\r\\n• 20+ pinball-machine assemblies, 600+ BOM components, and 2,500+ items.\\r\\n• Important story: Peter learned the planning mechanics before later relying on MRP automation as the answer.\\r\\nBedford Industries\\r\\n• Full MRP inside a 2+ year takeover and modernization of a failed prior implementation.\\r\\n• Learned the engine deeply on the fly: field behavior, timing, planning logic, and how the system actually produced recommendations.\\r\\n• Advanced Manufacturing plus work-order automation driven from sales and ship dates.\\r\\nCandyCo\\r\\n• Full MRP integrated with WMS, Manufacturing Mobile, complex BOMs, work orders, inventory movement, and quality.\\r\\nA-Tex use\\r\\nThree distinct planning stories show progression: underlying planning mechanics → deep MRP implementation knowledge → integrated MRP/WMS/manufacturing execution.\\r\\nH. Warehouse / Inventory / WMS / Physical Operations\\r\\nEarly warehouse-management foundation\\r\\n• Peter was a warehouse manager before becoming the ERP architect.\\r\\n• Understands receiving, storage, physical inventory, fulfillment, purchasing, and floor realities from the operator side.\\r\\n• Experience with physical counts/year-end inventory, inventory adjustments, and how quantity/cost changes affect the financials.\\r\\nCandyCo\\r\\n• Legacy ERP replacement with NetSuite WMS.\\r\\n• RF-SMART experience.\\r\\n• Strategic move from four locations toward one operating model / consolidation context.\\r\\n• Participated in strategic serial-number versus LPN decision-making.\\r\\n• Heavy WMS emulator customization, multi-building/blockable locations, LPN tracking, auto lot generation, Zebra labels, custom WMS processes, quality control, and complex BOM execution.\\r\\n• Configured handheld/mobile execution in Manufacturing Mobile to support the chosen inventory/handling architecture.\\r\\n• Worked hands-on with the floor team and printers/handheld execution when fulfillment readiness mattered.\\r\\nBeach House Group\\r\\n• Inventory and order management across a 25+ brand portfolio.\\r\\n• 3PL integrations and major retailer fulfillment environment.\\r\\nMesa Biotech\\r\\n• FDA-regulated lot tracking, item master design, and inventory architecture for accelerated diagnostic-kit manufacturing.\\r\\nA-Tex use\\r\\nBins, locations, barcoding, Zebra, lot/serial/LPN design, physical counts, receiving, picking, fulfillment, warehouse remap/consolidation, cost impact, and mobile execution are lived experience rather than theoretical knowledge.\\r\\nI. Order to Cash / Sales / CRM\\r\\nDesigns for Health\\r\\n• Project-managed and trained an 80+ person international NetSuite CRM rollout.\\r\\n• Owned dashboards, queries, and saved searches across departments.\\r\\nBedford Industries\\r\\n• CRM-sales integration.\\r\\n• Work-order automation driven from sales and ship dates, connecting front-end demand to production execution.\\r\\nBeach House Group\\r\\n• Order-management architecture across multiple consumer brands and large retail channels.\\r\\nTempest Telecom\\r\\n• CRM and warehouse-processing work in the same environment.\\r\\nA-Tex use\\r\\nSales process, CRM adoption, customer/order visibility, handoffs into fulfillment, and making sure sales demand becomes executable operations.\\r\\nJ. Reporting / KPIs / Dashboards / Saved Searches\\r\\nDesigns for Health\\r\\n• Owned dashboards, queries, saved searches, and departmental reporting.\\r\\nBenson Hill\\r\\n• Executive dashboards.\\r\\nJersey Jack\\r\\n• Controller-level inventory, BOM, procurement, and sales reporting.\\r\\nTrademark Construction\\r\\n• Project accounting and job-costing dashboards.\\r\\nSuja Juice\\r\\n• Custom reporting tied to costing and P&L variance work.\\r\\nA-Tex use\\r\\nWhen John raises slow or badly filtered saved searches, Peter can recognize several possible classes of issue without pretending to diagnose remotely: search design/filter logic, data volume, joins/formulas, permissions/audience, duplicate reports, or a search compensating for a broken upstream process.\\r\\nK. EDI / Integrations / Ecommerce / External Systems\\r\\nBeach House Group\\r\\n• Major retailer EDI and 3PL integrations.\\r\\n• Multiple Shopify/web-store connections.\\r\\n• Sourced Dell Boomi and designed integration flows/architecture.\\r\\nSuja Juice\\r\\n• EDI mapping.\\r\\nTrademark Construction\\r\\n• Custom API integration with Rapid ERP.\\r\\nBedford Industries\\r\\n• CRM-sales integration plus manufacturing automation.\\r\\nBroader integration toolkit\\r\\n• Dell Boomi, Celigo, SPS Commerce, EDI/cXML, REST, SOAP, SuiteTalk.\\r\\nA-Tex use\\r\\nPeter understands NetSuite as part of an enterprise ecosystem, not the only system in the building.\\r\\nL. Manufacturing / BOM / Work Orders / Quality\\r\\nJersey Jack Pinball\\r\\n• Rescue/reimplementation converting a flat-build BOM structure into modular manufacturing across 20+ assemblies.\\r\\n• 600+ BOM components.\\r\\nBedford Industries\\r\\n• Advanced Manufacturing, MRP, automated work orders, quality control, compliance tracking, project costing.\\r\\nCandyCo\\r\\n• Complex BOMs, automated work orders, Manufacturing Mobile, MRP/WMS integration, quality processes.\\r\\nMesa Biotech\\r\\n• FDA-regulated manufacturing architecture, lot tracking, item master, accelerated assembly environment.\\r\\nA-Tex use\\r\\nUseful if A-Tex expands beyond straightforward distribution into assembly, kitting, manufacturing, or more complex product structures.\\r\\nM. HR / Employee / Vendor Self-Service / Cross-Functional Systems\\r\\n• Worked with ADP integrations and the boundary between HR/payroll and ERP data.\\r\\n• Experience with Employee Center and role-based employee access.\\r\\n• Experience with Vendor Center and vendor self-service/access concepts.\\r\\n• Understands projects/project structures and how employees, vendors, approvals, time/cost, and accounting can intersect.\\r\\nA-Tex use\\r\\nDemonstrates enterprise-system breadth beyond Finance/Warehouse and supports conversations with HR around access, onboarding/offboarding, employee data, payroll interfaces, and role-based licensing.\\r\\nN. IT / Infrastructure / Security / Governance\\r\\n• Peter’s strength is ownership/governance, not pretending to be the deepest firewall engineer.\\r\\n• Director questions: what exists, who owns it, who supports it, what it costs, when it renews, whether coverage is adequate, and where risk/escalation lives.\\r\\n• Relevant inventory: users, devices, licenses, servers/infrastructure, offices, support contracts, third-party IT providers, access, renewal dates, and security ownership.\\r\\n• Experience spanning ERP plus Microsoft 365, Power Automate, SharePoint, Microsoft Graph, Power BI, Azure OpenAI/Claude/Python automation, giving Peter broader enterprise-technology context.\\r\\nA-Tex use\\r\\nThe role can govern IT vendors and technology standards without making Peter the technician for every specialty domain.\\r\\nO. Training / Change Management / Teaching\\r\\nDesigns for Health\\r\\n• Project-managed and trained 80+ international sales users on NetSuite CRM.\\r\\nCentric / implementation portfolio\\r\\n• Full delivery lifecycle repeatedly included discovery, testing, training, go-live, and stabilization.\\r\\nA-Tex use\\r\\nPrimary behavioral answer for 'Tell me about a time you had to teach people something' and evidence that Peter can translate technical change into business-user adoption.\\r\\nP. Rescue / Difficult Environments / Learning on the Fly\\r\\nBedford Industries\\r\\n• Took over a failed prior NetSuite Services implementation and modernized it over 2+ years.\\r\\n• Strong answer for inheriting something broken, learning a complex capability deeply, and stabilizing while improving.\\r\\nJersey Jack Pinball\\r\\n• Full rescue and reimplementation of complex manufacturing architecture.\\r\\nBeach House Group\\r\\n• Took over and rescued additional brands after initial brand launches.\\r\\nA-Tex use\\r\\nBehavioral triggers: difficult implementation, ambiguity, inherited technical debt, resistance, learning something new quickly, deciding what to preserve versus rebuild.\\r\\nQ. Industry / HVAC Relevance\\r\\nNSI / SUPCO HVAC via Centric\\r\\n• Direct HVAC/distribution experience.\\r\\n• Multi-brand engagement with a full new-company cutover into an existing NSI environment.\\r\\n• SuiteScript customization, workflow enhancements, and SuiteAnalytics reporting.\\r\\nA-Tex use\\r\\nUse early if they probe industry familiarity. Peter is not entering HVAC cold.\\r\\nR. Hands-On NetSuite / Admin Depth\\r\\n• Saved searches, workflows, SuiteAnalytics, SuiteScript, SuiteBuilder, role/access configuration, data migration, item master, inventory records, BOMs, WMS/mobile, labels, approvals, reporting, integrations, and troubleshooting.\\r\\n• The point is not to present Peter as the long-term ticket closer. The point is to establish that he can inspect ground truth himself, challenge vendors intelligently, and step into technical depth when the business needs it.\\r\\nFRIDAY QUESTION TRIGGERS — QUICK STORY ROUTING\\r\\n“Have you managed consultants?” → GrubMarket / Deloitte.\\r\\n“How deep are you with MRP?” → Jersey Jack → Bedford → CandyCo.\\r\\n“Have you worked with RF-SMART / WMS?” → CandyCo.\\r\\n“Have you managed a warehouse operation?” → early warehouse-management foundation + CandyCo floor leadership.\\r\\n“Do you understand purchasing?” → HairMax / Lexington → Jersey Jack → Bedford/CandyCo.\\r\\n“Have you dealt with costing/P&L issues?” → Beach House segmentation + Suja costing/revaluation/P&L variance + Jersey Jack valuation/costed BOM reporting.\\r\\n“Can you build executive reporting?” → Benson Hill + Jersey Jack + Designs for Health + Beach House.\\r\\n“Have you trained users?” → Designs for Health, 80+ international sales users.\\r\\n“Have you inherited something broken?” → Bedford or Jersey Jack.\\r\\n“Have you worked in HVAC?” → NSI / SUPCO HVAC.\\r\\n“Have you handled integrations?” → Beach House/Boomi/Shopify/EDI + Trademark API + Suja EDI + broader Celigo/SPS/REST/SOAP.\\r\\n“Have you owned NetSuite internally?” → GrubMarket + Beach House.\\r\\n“Have you managed people?” → Centric implementation teams + in-house leadership + CandyCo floor team.\\r\\n“What about licenses / ACS / NetSuite commercial relationship?” → contract/license ownership, renewal economics, ACS and partner-governance experience.\\r\\nDO NOT OVER-SOLUTION FRIDAY\\r\\nFor A-Tex-specific pain points, answer with pattern recognition and relevant proof, then ask questions.\\r\\nExample: serialized profitability / average costing\\r\\nGood posture: “I’ve worked through costing, inventory valuation, P&L variance, segmentation, serial/lot/LPN architecture, and Controller reporting in several environments. There are a few different classes of things that can create that symptom, but I’d want to see how A-Tex is transacting and reporting before calling the cause.”\\r\\nExample: saved searches\\r\\nGood posture: “I’ve owned saved-search and dashboard environments across multiple companies. I’d first separate design/filtering, data volume/performance, permissions/audience, and whether the search is compensating for a process or data problem upstream.”\\r\\nNEXT PASS — LATER, NOT NOW\\r\\nOnce the inventory is complete, compress this document into a one- or two-page live sidecar containing only:\\r\\n• 8–10 functional buckets.\\r\\n• 1–3 strongest company proof points per bucket.\\r\\n• Behavioral trigger questions.\\r\\n• A-Tex current-state notes.\\r\\n• Near-term vs long-term roadmap reminder.\\r\\n• Five or six anchor stories Peter can expand naturally.\\r\\nDo not compress until the experience inventory is complete.\"},{\"url\":\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1SXR_CaZs2r1_xjBl2_Dyg7mNsgFWojajt46PbrRWnFw/edit?usp=drivesdk\",\"title\":\"GCE_FRAMEWORK_HANDOFF_2026-09-21_MAIN_CHAT_RECONCILIATION\",\"id\":\"1SXR_CaZs2r1_xjBl2_Dyg7mNsgFWojajt46PbrRWnFw\",\"mime_type\":\"application/vnd.google-apps.document\",\"size\":\"4595\",\"file_or_folder\":\"file\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-21T19:17:26.461Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-09-21T19:18:29.852Z\",\"viewedByMeTime\":\"2026-09-21T19:18:29.852Z\",\"display_title\":\"GCE_FRAMEWORK_HANDOFF_2026-09-21_MAIN_CHAT_RECONCILIATION\",\"display_url\":\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1SXR_CaZs2r1_xjBl2_Dyg7mNsgFWojajt46PbrRWnFw/edit?usp=drivesdk\",\"parent_ids\":[\"1clV7UUxQUcnGLX21ossOWoHGBPs-pM77\"],\"shared\":false,\"content\":\"﻿GCE FRAMEWORK HANDOFF — MAIN CHAT RECONCILIATION\\r\\nDate: 2026-09-21\\r\\nSeat: Coda — Strategic Advisor / Governance\\r\\nPurpose: clean handoff from the long-running main framework chat into the new framework conversation\\r\\nStatus: CHECKPOINT / READY FOR CONTINUATION\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n1. AUTHORITY / IDENTITY DOCTRINE\\r\\n- IDENTITY != ROLE != LENS != PERSONALITY != AUTHORITY != MODEL.\\r\\n- MODEL != SEAT.\\r\\n- Don is final authority.\\r\\n- Seats retain stable organizational identity; model/runtime occupants remain replaceable.\\r\\n- Capability does not grant authority.\\r\\n- Coda owns governance, boundaries, canon/supersession, cross-seat reconciliation, and Don decision framing.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n2. DRIVE / ORGANIZATIONAL BOUNDARY\\r\\nCanonical root remains:\\r\\nGCE/\\r\\n- GCE_ORG\\r\\n- 03_Projects\\r\\n- 06_Frozen_State_and_Archive\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nHard boundary for this framework conversation:\\r\\n- Do not modify 03_Projects from framework reconciliation.\\r\\n- Projects own project truth and current implementation artifacts.\\r\\n- Framework receives project handoffs/evidence and extracts only reusable cross-project lessons outside Projects.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n3. NEW TECHNICAL STRATEGY RESEARCH LANES CREATED 2026-09-21\\r\\nUnder GCE_ORG / Executive Council / Technical Strategy / Systems Intelligence:\\r\\n- 01_Model_Labor_Intelligence\\r\\n- 02_Runtime_Provider_Intelligence\\r\\n- 03_Proving_Grounds\\r\\n- 04_Cross_Project_Methodology_Research\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nThese are non-project framework research surfaces.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n4. BLACK PEARL / MODEL LAB\\r\\nBlack Pearl was a bounded proving-ground experiment, not a new project.\\r\\nIt used a sparse frozen MLB packet to observe model behavior under uncertainty.\\r\\nPrimary outcome was a model labor map, not a model leaderboard.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nObserved role fits:\\r\\n- GPT-5.6 Sol: bounded senior analyst\\r\\n- Nex N2.5 Pro: grounded skeptic\\r\\n- Muse Spark 1.3: pattern analyst\\r\\n- Dots3: anomaly hunter\\r\\n- Grok 4.6: contradiction cross-examiner\\r\\n- Kimi K3 High: methodology challenger\\r\\n- Big Pickle: verifier / truth teller\\r\\n- GLM 5.1 / 5.3-class: architecture / integration\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nRaw research remains Technical Strategy evidence. Any seat/canon change requires separate governance promotion.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n5. RUNTIME / PROVIDER LESSONS\\r\\n- Cognitive suitability != operational suitability.\\r\\n- Route reliability, latency, rate limits, host, context continuity, and tool/file reliability matter separately.\\r\\n- Heavy stateful work should avoid silent model/runtime substitution mid-flight.\\r\\n- Kimi 2.7 high-speed stall vs K3-2-256K recovery and Big Pickle stateful takeover are retained as operating evidence.\\r\\n- Provider/runtime identity must not be confused with seat identity.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n6. CROSS-PROJECT METHODOLOGY LESSONS\\r\\nCandidate reusable laws:\\r\\n- POC / Discovery -> Design -> Architecture -> Validation -> Production.\\r\\n- Do not let an architect define an unfinished POC before explicit promotion.\\r\\n- Independence before convergence.\\r\\n- Preserve disagreement and provenance.\\r\\n- Verification != adjudication != final authority.\\r\\n- Different models/providers do not automatically create independent evidence.\\r\\n- Prefer progressive evidence where it improves signal.\\r\\n- Add persistent layers only when repeated operating need earns them.\\r\\n- Harden from real defects surgically rather than over-engineering hypothetical failures.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n7. RELATIONSHIP TO AEGIS / CAPO\\r\\nBlack Pearl does not create a competing architecture.\\r\\nIt provides empirical evidence for existing GCE/Aegis/Capo principles:\\r\\n- specialize / parallelize / converge\\r\\n- capability != authority\\r\\n- preserve provenance\\r\\n- controlled promotion\\r\\n- stage-gated verification\\r\\n- operator retains commitment authority\\r\\nAegis and Capo remain scoped/parked per their existing anchors unless separately promoted.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n8. PROJECT RECONCILIATION RULE\\r\\nProject does work -> project owns current truth -> project returns handoff/evidence -> framework reconciles outside Projects -> Technical Strategy extracts reusable method -> Strategic Governance decides canon/standard promotion.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nDo not overwrite project artifacts from the framework chat.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n9. EDGE CREW CONTEXT — ONLY WHAT FRAMEWORK NEEDS\\r\\nThe long chat temporarily used Edge Crew as a proving ground and generated useful framework lessons. Edge Crew implementation truth is intentionally not restated here.\\r\\nWhen needed, consume the latest project handoff and reconcile only the generalized lesson.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n10. DRIVE ARTIFACTS CURRENT AS OF THIS HANDOFF\\r\\nStrategic Governance:\\r\\n- GCE_GOOGLE_DRIVE_STRUCTURE_MANIFEST_CURRENT_2026-09-21\\r\\n- this handoff\\r\\n- existing Aegis / Capo anchors remain active scoping references\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nTechnical Strategy:\\r\\n- GCE_MODEL_LABOR_INTELLIGENCE_CURRENT_2026-09-21\\r\\n- GCE_RUNTIME_PROVIDER_INTELLIGENCE_CURRENT_2026-09-21\\r\\n- GCE_BLACK_PEARL_MODEL_LAB_RESEARCH_ANCHOR_2026-09-21\\r\\n- GCE_CROSS_PROJECT_METHODOLOGY_RESEARCH_CURRENT_2026-09-21\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n11. NEW CHAT OPENING INSTRUCTION\\r\\nLoad this handoff as the framework continuation anchor.\\r\\nDo not redesign the framework from scratch.\\r\\nFirst distinguish VERIFIED CURRENT STATE, RESEARCH EVIDENCE, PROPOSED DOCTRINE, and PROJECT-OWNED TRUTH.\\r\\nKeep Projects read-only unless Don explicitly opens a project reconciliation task.\\r\\nResume from the single highest-priority framework blocker or decision in front of Don.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nSTATUS: PROCEED\\r\\nCONFIDENCE: 97%\\r\\nREASON: the long main chat has been decomposed into framework research and governance artifacts without modifying project-owned work.\\r\\nNEXT GATE: new framework chat consumes this handoff; any project-specific update enters only through an explicit handoff/reconciliation step.\\r\\nDON INPUT NEEDED: No for continuity; Yes only for a material canon/authority change.\"},{\"url\":\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1_Gp3kyFvLbHV9TBDqILZ8JwtVJlsAcynTTZVPCQRtyg/edit?usp=drivesdk\",\"title\":\"A-Tex / Clavis — Director ERP & Enterprise Systems — Friday CFO Interview Prep — 2026-09-20\",\"id\":\"1_Gp3kyFvLbHV9TBDqILZ8JwtVJlsAcynTTZVPCQRtyg\",\"mime_type\":\"application/vnd.google-apps.document\",\"size\":\"16982\",\"file_or_folder\":\"file\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-20T18:48:09.614Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-09-22T16:03:33.517Z\",\"viewedByMeTime\":\"2026-09-22T16:03:33.517Z\",\"display_title\":\"A-Tex / Clavis — Director ERP & Enterprise Systems — Friday CFO Interview Prep — 2026-09-20\",\"display_url\":\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1_Gp3kyFvLbHV9TBDqILZ8JwtVJlsAcynTTZVPCQRtyg/edit?usp=drivesdk\",\"parent_ids\":[\"0AMKBhZSh1Ar5Uk9PVA\"],\"shared\":false,\"content\":\"﻿A-TEX / CLAVIS — DIRECTOR ERP & ENTERPRISE SYSTEMS\\r\\nFriday CFO Interview Prep — 2026-09-20\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nPURPOSE\\r\\nThis is the all-in-one operating artifact for A-Tex. It is not an interview script. It consolidates the current role thesis, verified public evidence, compensation framework, career logic, open questions, and Friday prep inputs so the next conversation can focus on how Peter should attack the 90-minute CFO interview with John Mutsko.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCURRENT STATUS\\r\\n• A-Tex / Apex Distribution is currently the strongest opportunity on the board.\\r\\n• Third interview: Friday, September 25, 2026 — 90 minutes with CFO John Mutsko.\\r\\n• Peter has already spoken with Kayleen and John.\\r\\n• Recruiter Tony owns the commercial process.\\r\\n• Peter has already told Tony:\\r\\n  - $220K base target.\\r\\n  - $200K base floor.\\r\\n  - relocation support.\\r\\n  - Director-level scope / authority.\\r\\n  - six-month review of scope, compensation, and operating model.\\r\\n• Compensation should not be negotiated directly with John unless John raises it. Tony remains the commercial channel.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nROLE THESIS\\r\\nThe role is no longer being treated as “NetSuite Architect” in the narrow sense.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCurrent working interpretation:\\r\\n• Internal Director of ERP / Enterprise Systems / IT ownership.\\r\\n• Own the NetSuite roadmap rather than simply execute tickets.\\r\\n• Own or coordinate integrations, EDI, RF-SMART, enterprise applications, outside consultants, and technology choices.\\r\\n• Become the internal escalation point for system/process questions.\\r\\n• Remove day-to-day system ownership burden from the CFO, CEO, HR/admin leadership, and operating teams.\\r\\n• Build a coherent roadmap and operating model.\\r\\n• Decide what should be built internally, what should be bought, and when outside consultants are justified.\\r\\n• Over time, build the right internal team around demonstrated need.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCORE CAREER SHIFT\\r\\nArchitect / consultant = solve the next problem.\\r\\nDirector / internal owner = decide which problems should be solved, in what order, by whom, with what budget, and own the outcome.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nThis is the career progression Peter has been looking for:\\r\\nSenior Architect / ERP Manager / Principal Consultant → Director-level enterprise owner → future VP / CIO path.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nThe value is not just a title. It is:\\r\\n• decision rights.\\r\\n• roadmap ownership.\\r\\n• resource planning.\\r\\n• vendor authority.\\r\\n• process ownership.\\r\\n• executive accountability.\\r\\n• enterprise-level continuity.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nWHAT A-TEX APPEARS TO BE BUYING FROM PETER\\r\\nNot merely more labor.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nThe incremental value of Peter over a NetSuite administrator or architect is:\\r\\n• cross-functional ERP judgment.\\r\\n• manufacturing / distribution operating experience.\\r\\n• executive communication.\\r\\n• ability to translate business process into systems roadmap.\\r\\n• ability to manage consultants rather than depend on them.\\r\\n• ability to reduce outside spend where internal ownership is more efficient.\\r\\n• ability to recognize where specialist help is actually required.\\r\\n• ability to create documentation, governance, sequencing, and institutional knowledge.\\r\\n• ability to build a team and operating function instead of becoming a permanent one-man ticket queue.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nA-TEX BUSINESS — VERIFIED PUBLIC CONTEXT\\r\\nClavis Capital Partners publicly describes A-Tex as:\\r\\n• Founded in 1976.\\r\\n• A leading provider of HVAC and related products.\\r\\n• Serving niche markets across the United States.\\r\\n• Exposure includes high-growth data center and critical infrastructure segments.\\r\\n• Based in Orlando, Florida, with offices throughout the Midwest and Southwest.\\r\\n• Clavis partnered with the founding family in 2024 to expand the company’s presence and deepen sector focus.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nClavis portfolio source:\\r\\nhttps://claviscp.com/potfolio.html\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nKroll transaction note:\\r\\n• Clavis investment / partnership announced around December 2024.\\r\\n• Purpose included ambitious growth goals and operational excellence.\\r\\nhttps://dev-kroll.netlify.app/en/transactions/financial-advisor-atex\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCLAVIS — VERIFIED OPERATING PROFILE\\r\\nClavis publicly describes itself as:\\r\\n• “Experienced operators, not financial engineers.”\\r\\n• Focused on US-based manufacturing, industrial services, business services, infrastructure, aerospace & defense, distribution and supply chain.\\r\\n• Providing capital AND operational assistance.\\r\\n• Looking for businesses near an inflection point and ready for the next level.\\r\\n• Looking for strong leadership teams that want a partner, not just capital.\\r\\n• Seeking sustainable businesses with defensible growth opportunities.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nPublic investment criteria:\\r\\nhttps://www.claviscp.com/investment-focus.html\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nPublic homepage:\\r\\nhttps://www.claviscp.com/\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nIMPORTANT INTERPRETATION\\r\\nThis supports a useful thesis, but it is still a thesis:\\r\\nA-Tex is not simply receiving money from a passive financial sponsor. Clavis appears to operate with an industrial-operator mindset and to professionalize / scale companies after investment.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nThat makes the A-Tex Director concept coherent:\\r\\n• growth creates systems complexity.\\r\\n• acquisitions / expansion increase integration and governance needs.\\r\\n• a founder / CFO-led ERP model becomes harder to sustain.\\r\\n• internal ownership becomes more valuable as the business scales.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nDo NOT treat Clavis as the employer or assume Clavis personally sets Peter’s paycheck. A-Tex is the employer. Clavis can influence governance, budgeting, incentive structures, leadership hiring, and value-creation priorities, but the exact degree of control over this role is not publicly established.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nNETSUITE TEAM / ADMIN HIRING SIGNAL\\r\\nPublic-market research surfaced A-Tex NetSuite administration hiring activity.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nImportant discipline:\\r\\n• Do not assume the administrator reports to Peter.\\r\\n• Do not assume the administrator requisition was paused pending Peter.\\r\\n• Do not assume A-Tex changed the role from admin to Director because candidates refused relocation.\\r\\n• Those are plausible explanations, not verified facts.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nWhat the signal does tell us:\\r\\nA-Tex has recognized a need for dedicated NetSuite capability below the executive / Director ownership layer.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nThat creates a clean question for Friday:\\r\\n“How are you thinking about the internal systems team underneath this role over the next 6–12 months?”\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nIf John independently describes an admin / analyst / technical resource reporting into the role, that becomes strong confirmation that Peter is being hired to build and lead a function rather than merely serve as the senior administrator.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCOMPENSATION ARCHITECTURE — WORKING MODEL\\r\\nBase salary:\\r\\n• Target: $220K.\\r\\n• Floor: $200K.\\r\\n• Build family budget on guaranteed $200K, not variable compensation.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nAnnual bonus:\\r\\n• Do not assume a specific percentage until the plan is provided.\\r\\n• Working planning range for a Director-level role: approximately 10–20% target, subject to company practice.\\r\\n• At $200K:\\r\\n  - 10% target = $20K.\\r\\n  - 15% target = $30K.\\r\\n  - 20% target = $40K.\\r\\n• Target bonus is not guaranteed payout.\\r\\n• Need written answers on:\\r\\n  - target percentage.\\r\\n  - company vs individual weighting.\\r\\n  - thresholds.\\r\\n  - caps.\\r\\n  - discretionary vs formula-driven treatment.\\r\\n  - first-year proration.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n401(k):\\r\\n• Peter intends to max his own employee contribution.\\r\\n• Kayleen previously described a company contribution believed to be around 5%, but the exact formula and eligibility must be confirmed in writing.\\r\\n• Do not describe it as a match unless the plan says it is a match.\\r\\n• Could be non-elective employer contribution, match, profit-sharing contribution, or another qualified-plan contribution.\\r\\n• Vesting must be confirmed.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nManagement incentive / long-term incentive:\\r\\nUse this as the umbrella question:\\r\\n“Is there a management incentive or long-term incentive program for key leadership?”\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nPossible forms:\\r\\n• annual profit-based cash.\\r\\n• EBITDA / value-creation incentive.\\r\\n• management incentive pool.\\r\\n• phantom equity.\\r\\n• true equity.\\r\\n• transaction / liquidity incentive.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nDo not call retained earnings an employee benefit. Retained earnings are company equity/accounting value kept in the business. Employee economic participation would need to be delivered through an actual compensation or ownership mechanism.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nSix-month review:\\r\\nPeter has already told Tony he wants a six-month review.\\r\\nWorking logic:\\r\\n• $200K can be the starting floor while the role and scope are proven.\\r\\n• $220K is the six-month alignment target if the actual remit is the Director-level ownership being discussed.\\r\\n• This is not framed as a normal merit increase.\\r\\n• It is a scope / market alignment review based on demonstrated responsibilities, outcomes, and authority.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCOMPENSATION QUESTIONS FOR OFFER STAGE\\r\\n1. Exact base.\\r\\n2. Bonus target percentage.\\r\\n3. Bonus formula and weighting.\\r\\n4. First-year bonus proration.\\r\\n5. 401(k) employee and employer mechanics.\\r\\n6. Vesting.\\r\\n7. Medical / dental / vision cost.\\r\\n8. PTO / holidays.\\r\\n9. Travel / vacation stipend or card Peter recalls Kayleen discussing (approximately $1,600/year — must be verified).\\r\\n10. Relocation package.\\r\\n11. Any management incentive / LTI.\\r\\n12. Six-month review language.\\r\\n13. Title and reporting line.\\r\\n14. Budget / hiring authority.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nMARKET COMP SIGNAL\\r\\nCurrent Florida NetSuite-specific research found:\\r\\n• WatchGuard Technologies — Director of ERP Systems, Miami — posted approximately $195K–$205K base; NetSuite-centered internal leadership.\\r\\n• Riveron — Technology & Innovation Associate Director (NetSuite), Miami — posted approximately $120K–$210K, but client-facing consulting rather than the internal-owner model Peter wants.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nInterpretation:\\r\\nA $200K A-Tex floor is not contradicted by current Florida market evidence for senior NetSuite / ERP leadership.\\r\\nDo not claim these roles are exact comparables; use them as external market anchors.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCAREER VALUE OF A-TEX\\r\\nWhy this opportunity can be more valuable than another Principal Consultant role even before cash:\\r\\n• Internal ownership instead of multi-client churn.\\r\\n• One company, one roadmap, one leadership team.\\r\\n• Opportunity to create the systems function rather than inherit endless ticket execution.\\r\\n• Ability to build repeatable governance and institutional knowledge.\\r\\n• Direct connection between technology decisions and enterprise value.\\r\\n• Potential PE-backed transformation story.\\r\\n• Stronger future narrative for VP / CIO roles.\\r\\n• Better structural separation between work and home.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nMHI COMPARISON — CORE DISTINCTION\\r\\nBoth MHI and A-Tex would be full-time W-2 roles with benefits.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nMHI:\\r\\n• still consulting.\\r\\n• multiple clients.\\r\\n• billable / utilization economics.\\r\\n• different industries and project contexts.\\r\\n• go-lives and deadlines can bleed into home life.\\r\\n• success is largely client-delivery based.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nA-Tex:\\r\\n• internal leadership.\\r\\n• one enterprise.\\r\\n• one platform / ecosystem.\\r\\n• one roadmap.\\r\\n• deeper long-term accountability.\\r\\n• opportunity to build team, governance, and direction.\\r\\n• work can be more deliberately separated from home once operating rhythm stabilizes.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nThis is not a guarantee of a 5 PM job. It is a structural difference in the work model.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nFAMILY / OPERATING MODEL — CURRENT PLAN\\r\\nThis is family planning, NOT an agreed A-Tex employment arrangement.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nIf starting October 1:\\r\\n• Oct 16–19: first home visit target.\\r\\n• Nov 13–29: desired long home block, including a request to work remotely from Mexico during week of Nov 16 and remain home through Thanksgiving.\\r\\n• Dec 18–Jan 4: desired Christmas / New Year home block.\\r\\n• Months 4–6: test a repeatable rhythm based on actual role performance and company flexibility.\\r\\n• Six-month target: define a more durable on-site cadence, potentially concentrated Orlando weeks rather than constant weekend travel.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nDo not present Mexico remote work, PTO, or future once-a-month travel as agreed facts.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nFRIDAY WITH JOHN — INPUTS, NOT SCRIPT\\r\\nThe goal is to behave like the Director John would be handing the system to.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nPeter does NOT need to prove:\\r\\n• every NetSuite checkbox.\\r\\n• exact accounting-list configuration.\\r\\n• every serialized inventory transaction detail.\\r\\n• that he can personally execute every admin / architect / developer task.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nPeter DOES need to demonstrate:\\r\\n• he can understand current state quickly.\\r\\n• he can establish priorities.\\r\\n• he can separate process problems from system problems.\\r\\n• he can determine what belongs internally vs externally.\\r\\n• he can govern vendors and consultants.\\r\\n• he can connect Finance, Operations, Warehouse, Sales and IT.\\r\\n• he can build a roadmap.\\r\\n• he can create decision rights.\\r\\n• he can create a resource plan.\\r\\n• he can own outcomes.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nNatural Friday posture:\\r\\n“Show me the business and system you have today, where you are trying to take it, what is painful, what is already in flight, and where ownership currently breaks down.”\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nThen Peter can reason with John rather than perform a technical audition.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nFIRST 30 / 60 / 90 — WORKING DIRECTOR FRAME\\r\\nDo not over-engineer this before the next prep conversation.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nFirst 30:\\r\\n• listen.\\r\\n• map people, processes, systems, vendors, contracts and pain points.\\r\\n• stabilize obvious risk.\\r\\n• understand current NetSuite / RF-SMART / EDI / integration landscape.\\r\\n• identify quick wins and major dependencies.\\r\\n• establish executive / stakeholder cadence.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nDays 31–60:\\r\\n• publish prioritized roadmap.\\r\\n• separate operational fixes from structural initiatives.\\r\\n• define ownership and decision rights.\\r\\n• rationalize consultant / vendor usage.\\r\\n• begin standards, documentation and governance.\\r\\n• assess internal staffing needs.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nDays 61–90:\\r\\n• execute highest-value roadmap items.\\r\\n• quantify cost / risk / throughput improvements.\\r\\n• finalize resource plan.\\r\\n• establish recurring operating metrics.\\r\\n• create 6–12 month roadmap with leadership.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nThis should evolve after John explains actual current state.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nOPEN QUESTIONS / GATES\\r\\nThese are the things that still need evidence:\\r\\n• Final title.\\r\\n• Reporting line.\\r\\n• Exact scope across ERP vs broader IT.\\r\\n• Whether infrastructure / helpdesk / cybersecurity are included.\\r\\n• Current internal systems team.\\r\\n• Whether an admin / analyst hire is planned underneath the role.\\r\\n• Existing consultant roster and spend.\\r\\n• Current NetSuite pain points.\\r\\n• Active projects already committed.\\r\\n• M&A / expansion expectations.\\r\\n• Budget authority.\\r\\n• Vendor-selection authority.\\r\\n• Hiring authority.\\r\\n• Bonus architecture.\\r\\n• 401(k) mechanics.\\r\\n• LTI / management incentive.\\r\\n• Relocation package.\\r\\n• Initial on-site expectation.\\r\\n• Future remote flexibility.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nDISCONFIRMERS\\r\\nThe opportunity weakens materially if Friday / offer stage reveals:\\r\\n• Director title with no decision rights.\\r\\n• permanent one-man admin / helpdesk expectation.\\r\\n• little or no budget authority.\\r\\n• no resource path.\\r\\n• compensation stuck materially below $200K.\\r\\n• role is primarily configuration / ticket execution.\\r\\n• vague ownership while CFO retains all real decisions.\\r\\n• permanently unsustainable on-site expectations with no path to a workable family model.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCURRENT THESIS\\r\\nThe evidence currently supports continued pursuit.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nWhat has moved positively:\\r\\n• Director-level ownership language.\\r\\n• roadmap responsibility.\\r\\n• CFO relief / handoff theme.\\r\\n• systems-function-building possibility.\\r\\n• A-Tex growth / critical-infrastructure exposure.\\r\\n• Clavis operator-led investment model.\\r\\n• external Florida NetSuite Director compensation evidence near $200K.\\r\\n• family calendar can be structured around the transition if flexibility is ultimately agreed.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nWhat has NOT yet been proven:\\r\\n• exact authority.\\r\\n• exact compensation.\\r\\n• exact team structure.\\r\\n• exact long-term incentive.\\r\\n• exact remote cadence.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nNEXT GATE\\r\\nUse this artifact as the source package for the next prep conversation.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nNext work:\\r\\nBuild the Friday CFO interview attack plan from this evidence:\\r\\n1. opening posture.\\r\\n2. questions that transfer system ownership from John to Peter.\\r\\n3. current-state → future-state conversation flow.\\r\\n4. 30/60/90 discussion.\\r\\n5. team / admin / consultant structure.\\r\\n6. PE / growth / infrastructure context.\\r\\n7. what NOT to over-answer technically.\\r\\n8. close and next-step positioning.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nSTATUS: PROCEED\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nFRIDAY CFO WORKING-SESSION AGENDA — OUTBOUND VERSION\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nOBJECTIVE\\r\\nSet the tone before Friday so John can enter the meeting knowing this will be a structured working session about current state, priorities, ownership, roadmap, and operating model — not an unstructured technical interview.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nOUTBOUND EMAIL DRAFT\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nSubject: Friday — working session outline\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nJohn —\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nLooking forward to Friday. Since we have 90 minutes, I wanted to put a little structure around the conversation so we can make the best use of the time and keep it focused on the areas that matter most.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nBased on our prior conversations, I thought the discussion could center on five things:\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Where the business and systems environment sit today — NetSuite, RF-SMART, EDI, integrations, outside partners, and anything already in flight.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Where you see the biggest pain points, risks, and priorities over the next 6–12 months.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n3. How ownership is structured today across Finance, Operations, Warehouse, Sales, outside consultants, and internal resources — and where you would most like that ownership to become cleaner.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n4. How I would think about the roadmap: immediate stabilization / quick wins, structural improvements, internal versus external resources, and the sequence for the first 90 days.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n5. What success looks like for the role from your perspective — both in the near term and as the company continues to grow.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nNo need to prepare anything formal. I’d rather use the time as a working conversation around what you are actually dealing with today and where you want the function to go.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nLooking forward to it.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nPeter\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nWHY THIS VERSION WORKS\\r\\n• It reflects topics already discussed with John rather than introducing a new presentation.\\r\\n• It gives John advance notice without creating homework.\\r\\n• It positions Peter at Director altitude: current state, ownership, priorities, roadmap, resourcing, outcomes.\\r\\n• It naturally invites John to bring current problems, active initiatives, and leadership context into the meeting.\\r\\n• It creates room for John to discuss whatever comes out of his upper-management meetings before Friday.\\r\\n• It avoids compensation, relocation, title negotiation, and the speculative admin-hiring theory.\\r\\n• It does not overcommit Peter to a prebuilt solution before he sees the actual current state.\\r\\n• It reduces the probability of the meeting collapsing into isolated NetSuite trivia.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nINTERNAL 90-MINUTE FLOW — NOT FOR EMAIL\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n0–10 minutes — Business / leadership context\\r\\n• What changed since the last conversation?\\r\\n• What came out of current leadership discussions?\\r\\n• What is creating urgency around this role now?\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n10–30 minutes — Current state\\r\\n• NetSuite environment.\\r\\n• RF-SMART / warehouse.\\r\\n• EDI.\\r\\n• integrations.\\r\\n• reporting / finance.\\r\\n• outside consultants / vendors.\\r\\n• known pain points.\\r\\n• projects already underway.\\r\\n• ownership gaps.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n30–50 minutes — Future state / priorities\\r\\n• What does John want off his plate?\\r\\n• What does leadership want different in 6–12 months?\\r\\n• What is operationally painful versus strategically limiting?\\r\\n• Which initiatives matter because of growth, scale, acquisitions, or infrastructure demands?\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n50–70 minutes — Peter’s operating model\\r\\n• process first, system second.\\r\\n• stabilize before redesign.\\r\\n• roadmap by business value / risk / dependency.\\r\\n• internal capability versus consulting support.\\r\\n• governance / decision rights.\\r\\n• team / resource architecture.\\r\\n• first 30 / 60 / 90 days.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n70–85 minutes — Role definition\\r\\n• decision authority.\\r\\n• reporting line.\\r\\n• internal team.\\r\\n• consultant/vendor ownership.\\r\\n• budget/resource expectations.\\r\\n• broader IT / enterprise systems boundary.\\r\\n• success measures.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n85–90 minutes — Close\\r\\n• summarize what Peter heard.\\r\\n• confirm where the role creates the most leverage.\\r\\n• identify remaining diligence / next step.\\r\\n• keep commercial terms with Tony unless John explicitly raises them.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nGUARDRAIL\\r\\nThe agenda is intended to create structure, not make John feel managed. Keep the email short, conversational, and collaborative. Do not attach a deck or detailed 30/60/90 before Friday unless John asks for one.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n2026-09-21 — JOHN FRIDAY PREP ANCHOR COMMIT\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nGUIDING OPERATING PRINCIPLE\\r\\nThe first 90 days are not the transformation. They are the runway that lets Peter assume ownership without breaking the business.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n30 DAYS — ONBOARD / GROUND\\r\\n• Learn the business before changing it.\\r\\n• Understand the people, departments, management team, warehouse, finance, sales, operations, vendors, consultants, IT support, NetSuite, RF-SMART, EDI, integrations, reporting, item/master-data structure, active projects, and known pain points.\\r\\n• Get introduced early to the consulting group, IT group, major vendors, and any other external partners.\\r\\n• Build the relationships immediately even if the associated work will not be tackled until day 30, 60, or later.\\r\\n• Meet department leaders and ask each for their 5–10 biggest systems/process problems.\\r\\n• Capture pain points without promising that every request becomes a project.\\r\\n• Build the real current-state map and validate the existing roadmap.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n60 DAYS — STABLE / QUICK WINS\\r\\n• Peter understands the NetSuite environment and operating context well enough to act with confidence.\\r\\n• Prioritize the department pain-point inventory by business impact, urgency, dependency, effort, and correct owner.\\r\\n• Attack high-impact / low-effort quick wins.\\r\\n• Prototype small fixes when appropriate.\\r\\n• Separate:\\r\\n  - work Peter should do personally.\\r\\n  - work that should be delegated internally.\\r\\n  - work that belongs with a consultant/vendor.\\r\\n  - work that should be deferred into the roadmap.\\r\\n• Establish a standing executive priority review with John or the appropriate sponsor.\\r\\n• Establish delivery / follow-up cadence with department owners.\\r\\n• Start taking recurring system ownership off John and other leaders.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n90 DAYS — ROADMAP EXECUTION\\r\\n• Peter is no longer onboarding.\\r\\n• Begin executing the larger roadmap.\\r\\n• Rationalize outside partners and vendors.\\r\\n• Review item/master-data/process architecture where it materially affects the roadmap.\\r\\n• Begin structural integration / EDI / RF-SMART / reporting / governance work.\\r\\n• Define the internal resource model and team needs.\\r\\n• Quantify early business wins and recurring pain removed.\\r\\n• Build the 6–12 month execution roadmap.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n90+ DAYS — REAL DIRECTOR PHASE\\r\\n• This is where the larger transformation begins.\\r\\n• Own prioritization, architecture direction, resource allocation, vendor strategy, governance, and executive communication.\\r\\n• Build internal capability where it creates leverage.\\r\\n• Use consultants as targeted capacity/specialists, not as owners of A-Tex institutional knowledge.\\r\\n• Maintain one coherent systems roadmap tied to business growth.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nDAY-ONE / WEEK-ONE MANAGEMENT MOVE\\r\\nRequest a short meeting with the management/director team.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nAsk each department:\\r\\n“Give me the 5–10 systems or process problems that frustrate you most, slow your team down, create risk, or stop you from getting information you need.”\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nThe point is not to create a 100-ticket backlog.\\r\\nThe point is to build a cross-functional pain inventory and identify:\\r\\n• immediate wins.\\r\\n• recurring root causes.\\r\\n• process problems masquerading as system problems.\\r\\n• technical debt.\\r\\n• reporting gaps.\\r\\n• ownership gaps.\\r\\n• vendor/consultant failures.\\r\\n• roadmap-level issues.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nPRIORITIZATION MODEL\\r\\nEvery request gets screened for:\\r\\n• business impact.\\r\\n• urgency.\\r\\n• dependency.\\r\\n• effort.\\r\\n• risk.\\r\\n• correct owner.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nPossible outcomes:\\r\\n• Peter solves it now.\\r\\n• Peter proves a concept quickly.\\r\\n• Internal resource owns it.\\r\\n• Consultant/vendor owns it under Peter’s direction.\\r\\n• Roadmap item.\\r\\n• Not worth doing / deprioritize.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nEXECUTIVE CADENCE\\r\\nTwo distinct rhythms:\\r\\n1. PRIORITY REVIEW\\r\\n   • Peter + John / executive sponsor.\\r\\n   • Agree what matters most.\\r\\n   • Reconcile department pressure against enterprise priorities.\\r\\n   • Confirm decisions / resources / escalations.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n2. DELIVERY REVIEW\\r\\n   • Peter + relevant department owners / resources.\\r\\n   • Check progress.\\r\\n   • Unblock decisions.\\r\\n   • Hold internal/external owners accountable.\\r\\n   • Close the loop so requests do not disappear.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCONSULTANT / VENDOR POST-MORTEM\\r\\nOne of the first important discovery questions:\\r\\n“If you have been through multiple consultants or partners, what happened?”\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nUnderstand:\\r\\n• why prior partners were hired.\\r\\n• where they succeeded.\\r\\n• where they failed.\\r\\n• scope problems.\\r\\n• architecture problems.\\r\\n• project-management problems.\\r\\n• turnover / continuity problems.\\r\\n• knowledge-transfer failures.\\r\\n• expectation gaps.\\r\\n• whether A-Tex lacked a strong internal owner.\\r\\n• which partners should stay.\\r\\n• which relationships should be changed or ended.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCORE THESIS\\r\\nIf A-Tex has historically moved from consultant to consultant, Peter becomes the continuity layer.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nConsultants do not own the company’s systems knowledge.\\r\\nA-Tex owns the knowledge.\\r\\nPeter owns the roadmap and directs outside expertise against bounded work.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nINTRODUCTIONS ARE A FIRST-30-DAY REQUIREMENT\\r\\nPeter should explicitly ask John:\\r\\n• Introduce me to the consulting group.\\r\\n• Introduce me to the IT group.\\r\\n• Introduce me to the key software / integration vendors.\\r\\n• Introduce me to the department leaders whose work depends most on the systems environment.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nThe objective is relationship creation, not immediate intervention.\\r\\nA problem can wait 60 days.\\r\\nThe relationship should not.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nFRIDAY JOHN WORKING SESSION — KEY CONFIRMATION TARGETS\\r\\nUse Friday to understand and validate:\\r\\n• what John needs off his plate.\\r\\n• where systems ownership currently breaks.\\r\\n• who owns what today.\\r\\n• which departments feel the most pain.\\r\\n• what external partners are doing now.\\r\\n• consultant history and failure modes.\\r\\n• where the business is going over the next 6–12 months.\\r\\n• what Clavis / ownership / executive leadership is prioritizing.\\r\\n• whether data-center / critical-infrastructure growth is central, emerging, or only one segment.\\r\\n• what role Peter is actually being hired to own.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nDo not force the hypothesis.\\r\\nAsk questions that allow John to confirm, correct, or reject it.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nFRIDAY POSTURE\\r\\nPeter is not selling a completed solution.\\r\\nPeter is demonstrating how he assumes ownership:\\r\\nlisten → map → prioritize → prove quick wins → establish governance → execute roadmap → build the function.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nSTATUS: PROCEED\\r\\nCONFIDENCE: 96%\\r\\nNEXT GATE: Build the dedicated Friday handoff / working-session prompt from this anchor and remove A-Tex tactical prep from the overloaded general job-search summary.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n2026-09-22 — COMMERCIAL / RELOCATION / FRIDAY DELTA\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nTONY COMMERCIAL SIGNAL\\r\\nPeter’s commercial framework to recruiter Tony LaBella:\\r\\n• $220K target base.\\r\\n• $200K floor.\\r\\n• relocation support.\\r\\n• Director-level scope / authority.\\r\\n• formal six-month review of scope, compensation, and operating structure.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nTony indicated he believes alignment is possible on the points raised and will provide further feedback before the next call.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nInterpretation:\\r\\n• Strong positive recruiter-level signal.\\r\\n• Not an approved offer.\\r\\n• Do not walk the $200K floor backward without new information.\\r\\n• Let Friday with John validate Director-level scope, then close commercial terms through Tony.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nRELOCATION WORKING STRUCTURE\\r\\n• Peter is currently in Las Vegas after transitioning out of Los Angeles.\\r\\n• Initial move is Peter only, not full-family relocation.\\r\\n• Preferred company support: direct-booked travel plus furnished temporary housing / Airbnb near the office.\\r\\n• Current ask: temporary housing through approximately Nov 1.\\r\\n• Peter will handle longer-term housing after establishing himself on the ground.\\r\\n• Peter is prepared to be on-site for the initial six-month period if package and mandate align.\\r\\n• If A-Tex wants an Orlando visit immediately after Friday to meet ownership / leadership, Peter can move quickly.\\r\\n• Working start target if the process closes: Oct 6–12.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nFRIDAY OWNERSHIP PRINCIPLE\\r\\nJohn’s role: sponsor the handoff and make the introductions.\\r\\nPeter’s role: own the relationships and work.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCritical early introductions:\\r\\n• consulting group.\\r\\n• IT group.\\r\\n• key vendors / integration partners.\\r\\n• department leaders.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nThe relationship should start immediately even if the associated work waits until day 30, 60, or later.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCore model:\\r\\nJohn sponsors → Peter owns relationship → Peter owns prioritization → Peter owns follow-through → John re-enters only when CFO authority is required.\"},{\"url\":\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1IbDb_a_VcRXbmfD04q0Zl2QneWvY67RYtC1n8uZRMfs/edit?usp=drivesdk\",\"title\":\"EC8_Runtime_Design_GLM5.1_v1.0\",\"id\":\"1IbDb_a_VcRXbmfD04q0Zl2QneWvY67RYtC1n8uZRMfs\",\"mime_type\":\"application/vnd.google-apps.document\",\"size\":\"10093\",\"file_or_folder\":\"file\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-06T15:52:56.904Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-09-06T15:52:58.752Z\",\"viewedByMeTime\":\"2026-09-06T15:52:58.752Z\",\"display_title\":\"EC8_Runtime_Design_GLM5.1_v1.0\",\"display_url\":\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1IbDb_a_VcRXbmfD04q0Zl2QneWvY67RYtC1n8uZRMfs/edit?usp=drivesdk\",\"parent_ids\":[\"1-2XT7kaaOIIu8aqjcGFIPF_ktPF0N9vQ\"],\"shared\":false,\"content\":\"﻿# EC8 AI Convergence — Independent Runtime / Model Design Pass v1.0\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n**Seat**: GLM 5.1 — Independent Design & Build authority (not architecture governance, not methodology ownership)\\r\\n**Date**: 2026-09-06\\r\\n**Posture**: Convert methodology requirements + Chiave Architecture v1.1 constraints + K1–K9 handoff into a concrete, cost-aware runtime design. No methodology modified. No architecture modified. No governance decisions.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n**Claim tags**: GIVEN | DERIVED | PROPOSED | UNKNOWN | OUTSIDE AUTHORITY\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n## 0. Source Constraints (Read, Not Authored)\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n| Source | Key Constraints Extracted |\\r\\n|--------|--------------------------|\\r\\n| Methodology v0.3.1 (S1) | N=5 analysts; 3 scheduled runs/slate (Overnight=1 framing call, Morning=26, PreGame=26); 53 calls/slate; ~795 calls/15-game day; ≥3 model families; L1=deterministic code; L2≠analyst family per pair; L3 outside pool; normalization blind; no on-demand |\\r\\n| Chiave Architecture v1.1 | PR-1 family taxonomy = provider→lineage→model→version; PR-2 registry pre-flight check; EX-2 heterogeneity machine-checkable; FB-1/2 L3 fallback pre-approved; CK-3 crash-restart ≤2/analyst/run; I9 provenance from execution metadata only |\\r\\n| Reconciliation v0.2 | DR-14↑ family taxonomy undefined (blocks EX-2 machine-check); DR-22 G4 registries missing; DR-24 contamination mechanisms unspecified; Phase 0/1 startable, Phase 2 blocked |\\r\\n| Prompt package 01–09 | 9 distinct system prompts; structured JSON output required per prompt; temperature 0.3–0.7 range recommended per persona |\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n## 1. Model-Family Taxonomy (PROPOSED — per PR-1, awaiting Chiave formal pin via DR-14↑)\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nHierarchy: `provider_org → foundation_lineage (family_id) → model → version`\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n| family_id | provider_org | Foundation Lineage | Bedrock Available |\\r\\n|-----------|-------------|-------------------|-------------------|\\r\\n| `anthropic-claude` | Anthropic | Claude (Transformer, RLHF/Constitutional) | YES |\\r\\n| `meta-llama` | Meta | Llama (Dense+MoE Transformer, open weights) | YES |\\r\\n| `mistral` | Mistral AI | Mistral (Sparse MoE Transformer) | YES |\\r\\n| `deepseek` | DeepSeek | DeepSeek (MoE Transformer, DeepSeek-specific) | YES |\\r\\n| `amazon-nova` | Amazon | Nova (Amazon-built Transformer) | YES |\\r\\n| `minimax` | MiniMax | MiniMax (MoE Transformer) | YES |\\r\\n| `google-gemma` | Google | Gemma (Dense Transformer, open weights) | YES |\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n**Distinct orgs sharing a lineage do not satisfy EX-2** (PR-1). Same org with distinct lineages may. All seven families above are distinct lineages from distinct orgs — they satisfy EX-2 pairwise.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n**UNKNOWN**: Whether Kimi/Chiave accept this exact taxonomy. DR-14↑ blocks the machine-check until formally pinned. This design proceeds against this proposed taxonomy as the compliance criterion.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n## 2. Bedrock Pricing Reference (GIVEN — us-east-1, standard tier, 2026-09-06)\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n| Model | family_id | Input/1M tok | Output/1M tok | Structured Output | Context Window |\\r\\n|-------|-----------|-------------|--------------|-------------------|---------------|\\r\\n| Claude 3.5 Sonnet v2 | anthropic-claude | $3.00 | $15.00 | JSON mode | 200K |\\r\\n| Claude 3 Haiku | anthropic-claude | $0.80 | $4.00 | JSON mode | 200K |\\r\\n| Llama 3.3 70B Instruct | meta-llama | $0.36 | $1.46 | Tool call | 128K |\\r\\n| Llama 3.1 8B Instruct | meta-llama | $0.12 | $0.24 | Tool call | 128K |\\r\\n| Mistral Small 24B | mistral | $0.08 | $0.24 | JSON mode | 32K |\\r\\n| Mistral Large 3 | mistral | $0.50 | $1.50 | JSON mode | 128K |\\r\\n| DeepSeek v3.2 | deepseek | $0.62 | $1.85 | JSON mode | 128K |\\r\\n| Amazon Nova Pro | amazon-nova | $0.80 | $3.20 | JSON mode | 300K |\\r\\n| Amazon Nova Lite | amazon-nova | $0.06 | $0.24 | JSON mode | 300K |\\r\\n| Amazon Nova Micro | amazon-nova | $0.035 | $0.14 | JSON mode | 128K |\\r\\n| MiniMax M2.5 | minimax | $0.30 | $1.20 | JSON mode | 1M |\\r\\n| Gemma 3 27B IT | google-gemma | $0.23 | $0.38 | JSON mode | 128K |\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n**UNKNOWN**: Exact Bedrock model IDs for some models (see §4 manifest). Claude 3.5 Sonnet v2 pricing shown is standard on-demand; extended-access variant is $6/$30. Llama 4 model IDs and pricing not yet confirmed on Bedrock (checked 2026-09-06 — Llama 4 section present but model rows not rendered in pricing page scrape). Pricing may have changed since scrape.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n## 3. Token Estimates (DERIVED — per-prompt, single-matchup slate)\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n| Stage | Prompt ID | Est. Input Tokens | Est. Output Tokens | Reasoning |\\r\\n|-------|-----------|------------------|-------------------|-----------|\\r\\n| Framing | P01 | 4,000 | 2,000 | Raw slate data → fixed schema. Input is pre-fetched data (~3K) + system prompt (~1K). Output is populated JSON. |\\r\\n| Analyst 1st pass | P02–P06 | 6,000 | 1,500 | System prompt (~2K) + framing schema (~2K) + gap status map (~2K). First pass: no evidence yet. |\\r\\n| Analyst re-analysis | P02–P06 | 20,000 | 2,000 | System prompt + framing + full evidence package (~10K cumulative) + first-pass context carried in session. This is the token-heavy call (OB-1). |\\r\\n| L2 Validation | P07 | 12,000 | 1,000 | System prompt (~2K) + analyst output (~2K) + evidence package (~6K) + framing (~2K). No raw confidence. |\\r\\n| L3 Validation | P08 | 16,000 | 1,500 | System prompt (~2K) + analyst output (~2K) + L1 report (~0.5K) + L2 report (~1K) + evidence (~8K) + framing (~2K). Red-team scope is broader. |\\r\\n| Normalization | P09 | 3,000 | 500 | System prompt (~2K) + 4 structured inputs (~1K). Tightly constrained. |\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n**Note**: Analyst re-analysis carries the full first-pass context (EX-3: same session). The 20K input estimate accounts for evidence accumulation across runs (IF-2 cumulative delivery). Pre-Game runs may carry more accumulated evidence than Morning runs.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n---\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n## 4. Runtime Assignment Matrix — Profile A: Cost-Optimized\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n**Goal**: Minimum sufficient intelligence. Cheapest model that reliably satisfies the contract for each stage.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n### 4.1 Primary Assignments\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n| Stage | Prompt ID | Calls/Run | Required Capability | Proposed Model | family_id | Bedrock Model ID | Structured Output | Context Req | Reasoning Level | Temp | Timeout | Retry |\\r\\n|-------|-----------|-----------|--------------------|---------------|-----------|------------------|-------------------|-------------|----------------|------|---------|-------|\\r\\n| Framing | P01 | 1 | Schema fill, zero discretion | Amazon Nova Micro | amazon-nova | `amazon.nova-micro-v1:0` | JSON mode | 128K | Low | 0.1 | 30s | 1 |\\r\\n| Analyst A (Situational) | P02 | 1+1 | Contextual reasoning, evidence citation | DeepSeek v3.2 | deepseek | `deepseek.deepseek-v3:0` | JSON mode | 128K | Medium | 0.5 | 120s | 1 |\\r\\n| Analyst B (Form) | P03 | 1+1 | Trend/regression reasoning | Meta Llama 3.3 70B | meta-llama | `meta.llama3-3-70b-instruct-v1:0` | Tool call | 128K | Medium | 0.4 | 120s | 1 |\\r\\n| Analyst C (Structural) | P04 | 1+1 | Matchup/tactical reasoning | Mistral Small 24B | mistral | `mistral.mistral-small-2407-v1:0` | JSON mode | 32K | Medium | 0.3 | 120s | 1 |\\r\\n| Analyst D (Market) | P05 | 1+1 | Market/behavioral reasoning | MiniMax M2.5 | minimax | `minimax.m2-5-v1:0` | JSON mode | 1M | Medium | 0.6 | 120s | 1 |\\r\\n| Analyst E (Micro) | P06 | 1+1 | Process metrics, statistical reasoning | Amazon Nova Pro | amazon-nova | `amazon.nova-pro-v1:0` | JSON mode | 300K | Medium-High | 0.3 | 120s | 1 |\\r\\n| L2 Validation | P07 | 5 | Factual checking, classification | Per-analyst (see §4.2) | varies | varies | JSON mode | varies | Medium | 0.2 | 90s | 1 |\\r\\n| L3 Validation | P08 | 5 | Red-team, missed evidence | Google Gemma 3 27B IT | google-gemma | `google.gemma3-27b-it-v1:0` | JSON mode | 128K | Medium | 0.3 | 120s | 1 |\\r\\n| Normalization | P09 | 5 | 4-input→5-tier, constrained | Amazon Nova Micro | amazon-nova | `amazon.nova-micro-v1:0` | JSON mode | 128K | Low | 0.1 | 30s | 1 |\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n**Calls/Run notation**: \\\"1+1\\\" = 1 first-pass + 1 re-analysis (same session, two provider requests per EX-3). L2/L3/Normalization = 5 calls per run (one per analyst).\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n### 4.2 L2 Per-Analyst Assignment (EX-2: different family from target analyst)\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n| Validates Analyst | Target family_id | L2 Model | L2 family_id | Bedrock Model ID | Input/1M | Output/1M |\\r\\n|-------------------|-----------------|----------|-------------|------------------|----------|-----------|\\r\\n| A (Situational) | deepseek | Amazon Nova Lite | amazon-nova | `amazon.nova-lite-v1:0` | $0.06 | $0.24 |\\r\\n| B (Form) | meta-llama | Amazon Nova Lite | amazon-nova | `amazon.nova-lite-v1:0` | $0.06 | $0.24 |\\r\\n| C (Structural) | mistral | Amazon Nova Lite | amazon-nova | `amazon.nova-lite-v1:0` | $0.06 | $0.24 |\\r\\n| D (Market) | minimax | Amazon Nova Lite | amazon-nova | `amazon.nova-lite-v1:0` | $0.06 | $0.24 |\\r\\n| E (Micro) | amazon-nova | Mistral Small 24B | mistral | `mistral.mistral-small-2407-v1:0` | $0.08 | $0.24 |\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n**Rationale**: Nova Lite ($0.06/$0.24) is the cheapest capable model for L2's factual-checking + G4 classification task. Used for all analysts where family constraint permits. For Analyst E (amazon-nova family), Mistral Small 24B is the cheapest cross-family option.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n### 4.3 Heterogeneity Check (EX-2)\\r\\n\\r\\n\\