de: Peter can bridge the ERP transactional / control layer with the emerging AI / automation layer without becoming the person who has to build every component personally.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCLOSE / HOME-RUN MESSAGE\\r\\nPeter can run the engagement today, understands the technology deeply enough to lead it intelligently, can relate to the client, and has upside into where the practice is going next with AI-enabled ERP delivery.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nPREP METHOD FROM HERE\\r\\nKeep prep lightweight and practical. Use the one-sheet rail as the visible control surface. Practice through conversational case prompts. Challenge wording directly: flag when Peter drops into the wrong role altitude, over-architects, uses weak NetSuite language, skips lifecycle steps, or says something that creates unnecessary ownership. The goal is not to memorize a script; it is to make the Principal Consultant operating pattern automatic.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n2026-09-16 — LIVE INTERVIEW / PIPELINE HANDOFF\\r\\n- PRIMARY MISSION TODAY: Myers-Holum Principal Consultant final interview with Andrew, 10:00–11:30 AM Pacific. Excalibur room is secured through noon, so the interview environment is protected.\\r\\n- MORNING WARM-UP: quick recruiter conversation with Meghan / The Planet Group around 7:30 AM Pacific. Treat as a conversational warm-up and market check, not a deep interview; keep it short so the 10:00 AM Myers-Holum window stays protected.\\r\\n- PRINCIPAL CONSULTANT POSTURE REMAINS: own lifecycle coherence, validate the sold scope and pre-sales solution handoff, pressure-test assumptions/dependencies/risks, establish workstreams/owners/sequence/governance/health, and descend into architecture/config only where materially necessary.\\r\\n- SIX-LANE COMMAND BOARD: Discovery -> Process -> Solution -> Delivery -> Governance -> Health / Commercial. Use this as the visible interview rail; do not read a script.\\r\\n- LANGUAGE GUARD: use NetSuite / consulting language with Andrew, not GCE/AI-framework jargon. Translate artifact -> handoff package / SOW / requirements; runtime -> delivery process/cadence; routing -> workstream ownership / specialist handoff; governance substrate -> project governance / decision process.\\r\\n- MYERS-HOLUM PRE-SALES SEAM: current best framing is to start from the sold scope / pre-sales solution handoff produced by the internal sales/solution-architecture function, validate it against actual business reality, and escalate materially mis-scoped items rather than silently absorbing them.\\r\\n- ATEX: follow-up is Thursday morning around 7:00 AM Pacific. Current position remains lower priority unless the onsite/Orlando structure becomes materially more family-compatible; do not let ATEX distract from Myers-Holum today.\\r\\n- OASIS SOLUTIONS: first 30-minute exploratory/get-to-know-you call is being scheduled in broad availability windows after Myers-Holum; treat it as a back-seat opportunity that must prove itself.\\r\\n- RF-SMART: next interview / conversation is next Tuesday around 11:00 AM Pacific. Remains a strong comparator because of the internal/lifestyle/stability model.\\r\\n- PLANET GROUP: warm senior channel remains active. Meghan is useful for market demand and funded NetSuite work; do not over-architect without a concrete requirement.\\r\\n- CURRENT DECISION ORDER: Myers-Holum first; then ATEX/Oasis/RF-SMART as evidence arrives. Do not chase weak or vague processes just to create activity.\\r\\n- INTERVIEW DAY RULE: no hotel/budget/admin problem-solving inside the 90 minutes before Andrew unless it is a true emergency. Prep, protein/light breakfast, brief sunlight/movement, then focus.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n2026-09-20 — A-TEX DIRECTOR / CLAVIS ANCHOR\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCURRENT PRIORITY\\r\\nA-Tex is now the primary opportunity on the active board, subject to Friday’s CFO interview and final commercial terms. RF-SMART remains the main wildcard comparator. Myers-Holum remains alive but is structurally less aligned with the current family/career objective because it preserves the consulting operating model.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCURRENT A-TEX GATE\\r\\n• Third interview: Friday, September 25, 2026 — 90 minutes with CFO John Mutsko.\\r\\n• Commercial channel: Tony.\\r\\n• Base target already communicated: $220K.\\r\\n• Base floor already communicated: $200K.\\r\\n• Requested: relocation support, Director-level scope/authority, and six-month scope/comp/operating-model review.\\r\\n• Friday should be treated as a Director-to-CFO working session, not a NetSuite configuration audition.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nROLE THESIS\\r\\nPeter’s target seat is internal Director of ERP / Enterprise Systems ownership:\\r\\n• own the NetSuite roadmap.\\r\\n• coordinate/own integrations, EDI, RF-SMART and enterprise applications.\\r\\n• govern consultants/vendors.\\r\\n• connect Finance, Operations, Warehouse, Sales and technology.\\r\\n• determine build vs buy vs outsource.\\r\\n• create roadmap, governance, decision rights and resource plan.\\r\\n• build the internal systems function over time.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCLAVIS / A-TEX PUBLIC EVIDENCE\\r\\n• Clavis partnered with the A-Tex founding family in 2024.\\r\\n• A-Tex serves HVAC, data-center and critical-infrastructure markets.\\r\\n• Clavis publicly positions itself as operator-led, providing capital plus operational assistance to US industrial/manufacturing/distribution businesses near an inflection point.\\r\\n• This supports — but does not prove — the thesis that A-Tex is professionalizing systems/leadership as part of the next growth stage.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nDISCIPLINE\\r\\nDo not assume:\\r\\n• the NetSuite admin role reports to Peter.\\r\\n• the admin hire is paused pending Peter.\\r\\n• Clavis directly sets Peter’s compensation.\\r\\n• future Mexico remote work or once-per-month travel is already agreed.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nThese are questions to validate, not facts.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCOMPENSATION WORKING MODEL\\r\\n• Build family economics on $200K guaranteed base.\\r\\n• $220K remains target / six-month alignment point if scope proves out.\\r\\n• Bonus target must be confirmed in writing; planning range can remain 10–20% until actual plan is provided.\\r\\n• Max employee 401(k) contribution; employer mechanics/vesting must be confirmed.\\r\\n• Ask whether leadership has a management incentive / long-term incentive program (profit-based cash, EBITDA/value creation, phantom equity, true equity, or transaction incentive).\\r\\n• Keep retained earnings concept separate from employee compensation.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nDEDICATED SOURCE PACKAGE\\r\\nA-Tex / Clavis — Director ERP & Enterprise Systems — Friday CFO Interview Prep — 2026-09-20\\r\\nhttps://docs.google.com/document/d/1_Gp3kyFvLbHV9TBDqILZ8JwtVJlsAcynTTZVPCQRtyg/edit\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nNEXT GATE\\r\\nUse the dedicated A-Tex artifact to build the Friday attack plan:\\r\\n1. opening posture.\\r\\n2. questions that transfer system ownership from John to Peter.\\r\\n3. current-state → future-state conversation flow.\\r\\n4. 30/60/90.\\r\\n5. team/admin/consultant structure.\\r\\n6. Clavis/growth context.\\r\\n7. technical depth guardrails.\\r\\n8. close and next-step positioning.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nSTATUS: PROCEED\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n2026-09-22 — JOB SEARCH CURRENT STATUS ANCHOR\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nA-TEX / APEX DISTRIBUTION — CURRENT LEAD\\r\\n• Friday Sep 25: 90-minute working session with CFO John Mutsko.\\r\\n• Role has evolved from NetSuite Architect into a probable Director-level ERP / Enterprise Systems / IT ownership seat, subject to confirmation Friday.\\r\\n• Commercial ask to recruiter Tony LaBella: $220K target base, $200K floor, relocation support, Director-level scope/authority, and formal six-month review of scope/comp/operating model.\\r\\n• Tony indicated he believes alignment is possible on the points raised and will provide further feedback before the next call. Treat as strong recruiter-level alignment, not a written offer.\\r\\n• Peter has sent John a Friday working-session agenda centered on business direction, current systems environment, department pain points, ownership/resources/vendors, roadmap/sequencing, and definition of success.\\r\\n• Operating thesis: John sponsors the handoff; Peter owns relationships, prioritization, roadmap, vendors, governance, and follow-through.\\r\\n• First-30-day relationship move: introductions to consulting group, IT group, key vendors, and department leaders immediately, even if associated work waits until day 30/60/90.\\r\\n• Relocation working ask: company-booked travel plus short-term furnished housing/Airbnb near the office through approximately Nov 1, while Peter handles longer-term housing after getting established.\\r\\n• Peter is prepared to commit to the initial six-month on-site period if the final package and mandate align.\\r\\n• Target start window if closed: roughly Oct 6–12.\\r\\n• A-Tex is currently the front-runner based on scope, ownership, guaranteed compensation potential, family culture, and career trajectory.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nMHI / MYERS-HOLUM — STILL ALIVE, SECONDARY\\r\\n• Final Principal Consultant interview completed; case/BRD follow-up sent.\\r\\n• No final outcome recorded yet.\\r\\n• Working comp expectation remains materially more variable than A-Tex; prior discussions suggested lower base with upside through variable compensation.\\r\\n• Structural concern remains consulting model: concurrent clients, utilization/billability, context switching, and year-end delivery pressure.\\r\\n• Peter remains fully engaged but increasingly prefers internal enterprise ownership over professional-services delivery.\\r\\n• MHI remains useful as leverage / fallback / market confirmation, but is not current lead.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nOASIS SOLUTIONS — MAJOR COMP STRUCTURE CONCERN\\r\\n• HR conversation with Nichelle completed Sep 22; 2026 Benefits Guide received and reviewed.\\r\\n• Base discussed around $160K; Peter perceived $160K as already near the upper end of their comfort.\\r\\n• Profit-share is not treated as guaranteed compensation.\\r\\n• Benefit guide states Gross Profit Share is available only after one year of employment; first eligible year is prorated; employee must still be employed at payment; pool depends on company gross-profit and revenue targets; distribution depends on assigned vested Profit Share Units and other factors.\\r\\n• Current valuation rule: treat Oasis profit share as $0 until Oasis provides Peter’s specific unit allocation plus historical payout economics.\\r\\n• Oasis 401(k): 3% non-elective employer contribution.\\r\\n• Employee-only medical has low/no premium options; family coverage is materially more expensive.\\r\\n• Current conclusion: Oasis is economically far behind A-Tex on guaranteed base and does not currently close the gap through its profit-share design.\\r\\n• Recruiter James Delaney communication plan: acknowledge review, raise concerns around structure/timing/market alignment, ask for written clarity on actual profit-share economics, and defer phone conversation until Peter’s cell-provider switch is complete.\\r\\n• Do not burn the bridge, but do not count opaque/contingent upside.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nRF-SMART — PAUSED / REASSESSING\\r\\n• Another one-hour interview had been scheduled for Sep 22; Peter chose to push it back for a short reset.\\r\\n• Reschedule language: personal scheduling conflict.\\r\\n• Availability offered: Wednesday open all day; Thursday 7 AM–12 PM PT; next Monday–Wednesday 7 AM–5 PM PT.\\r\\n• Confirmed operating advantage: only about one office visit per month.\\r\\n• Decision rule: RF-SMART becomes a serious challenger to A-Tex only if it can approach A-Tex on guaranteed base AND role altitude / decision rights.\\r\\n• If RF-SMART can reach roughly $200K+ guaranteed with true Director-level ownership and monthly-office cadence, the flexibility becomes a major family advantage.\\r\\n• If scope remains architect / bounded mature-function role or comp lands materially below A-Tex, A-Tex remains stronger.\\r\\n• Next RF-SMART objective: determine real level, guaranteed base, authority, and progression before assigning priority.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCURRENT ORDER\\r\\n1. A-Tex / Apex — lead, conditional on Friday scope confirmation and commercial close.\\r\\n2. RF-SMART — potential challenger if comp + authority rise to A-Tex level.\\r\\n3. MHI — viable but structurally less aligned with desired internal-owner path.\\r\\n4. Oasis — major guaranteed-comp and incentive-structure gap; keep warm only pending hard numbers.\"},{\"url\":\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1_Gp3kyFvLbHV9TBDqILZ8JwtVJlsAcynTTZVPCQRtyg/edit?usp=drivesdk\",\"title\":\"A-Tex / Clavis — Director ERP & Enterprise Systems — Friday CFO Interview Prep — 2026-09-20\",\"id\":\"1_Gp3kyFvLbHV9TBDqILZ8JwtVJlsAcynTTZVPCQRtyg\",\"mime_type\":\"application/vnd.google-apps.document\",\"size\":\"16982\",\"file_or_folder\":\"file\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-20T18:48:09.614Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-09-22T16:03:33.517Z\",\"viewedByMeTime\":\"2026-09-22T16:03:33.517Z\",\"display_title\":\"A-Tex / Clavis — Director ERP & Enterprise Systems — Friday CFO Interview Prep — 2026-09-20\",\"display_url\":\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1_Gp3kyFvLbHV9TBDqILZ8JwtVJlsAcynTTZVPCQRtyg/edit?usp=drivesdk\",\"parent_ids\":[\"0AMKBhZSh1Ar5Uk9PVA\"],\"shared\":false,\"content\":\"﻿A-TEX / CLAVIS — DIRECTOR ERP & ENTERPRISE SYSTEMS\\r\\nFriday CFO Interview Prep — 2026-09-20\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nPURPOSE\\r\\nThis is the all-in-one operating artifact for A-Tex. It is not an interview script. It consolidates the current role thesis, verified public evidence, compensation framework, career logic, open questions, and Friday prep inputs so the next conversation can focus on how Peter should attack the 90-minute CFO interview with John Mutsko.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCURRENT STATUS\\r\\n• A-Tex / Apex Distribution is currently the strongest opportunity on the board.\\r\\n• Third interview: Friday, September 25, 2026 — 90 minutes with CFO John Mutsko.\\r\\n• Peter has already spoken with Kayleen and John.\\r\\n• Recruiter Tony owns the commercial process.\\r\\n• Peter has already told Tony:\\r\\n  - $220K base target.\\r\\n  - $200K base floor.\\r\\n  - relocation support.\\r\\n  - Director-level scope / authority.\\r\\n  - six-month review of scope, compensation, and operating model.\\r\\n• Compensation should not be negotiated directly with John unless John raises it. Tony remains the commercial channel.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nROLE THESIS\\r\\nThe role is no longer being treated as “NetSuite Architect” in the narrow sense.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCurrent working interpretation:\\r\\n• Internal Director of ERP / Enterprise Systems / IT ownership.\\r\\n• Own the NetSuite roadmap rather than simply execute tickets.\\r\\n• Own or coordinate integrations, EDI, RF-SMART, enterprise applications, outside consultants, and technology choices.\\r\\n• Become the internal escalation point for system/process questions.\\r\\n• Remove day-to-day system ownership burden from the CFO, CEO, HR/admin leadership, and operating teams.\\r\\n• Build a coherent roadmap and operating model.\\r\\n• Decide what should be built internally, what should be bought, and when outside consultants are justified.\\r\\n• Over time, build the right internal team around demonstrated need.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCORE CAREER SHIFT\\r\\nArchitect / consultant = solve the next problem.\\r\\nDirector / internal owner = decide which problems should be solved, in what order, by whom, with what budget, and own the outcome.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nThis is the career progression Peter has been looking for:\\r\\nSenior Architect / ERP Manager / Principal Consultant → Director-level enterprise owner → future VP / CIO path.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nThe value is not just a title. It is:\\r\\n• decision rights.\\r\\n• roadmap ownership.\\r\\n• resource planning.\\r\\n• vendor authority.\\r\\n• process ownership.\\r\\n• executive accountability.\\r\\n• enterprise-level continuity.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nWHAT A-TEX APPEARS TO BE BUYING FROM PETER\\r\\nNot merely more labor.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nThe incremental value of Peter over a NetSuite administrator or architect is:\\r\\n• cross-functional ERP judgment.\\r\\n• manufacturing / distribution operating experience.\\r\\n• executive communication.\\r\\n• ability to translate business process into systems roadmap.\\r\\n• ability to manage consultants rather than depend on them.\\r\\n• ability to reduce outside spend where internal ownership is more efficient.\\r\\n• ability to recognize where specialist help is actually required.\\r\\n• ability to create documentation, governance, sequencing, and institutional knowledge.\\r\\n• ability to build a team and operating function instead of becoming a permanent one-man ticket queue.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nA-TEX BUSINESS — VERIFIED PUBLIC CONTEXT\\r\\nClavis Capital Partners publicly describes A-Tex as:\\r\\n• Founded in 1976.\\r\\n• A leading provider of HVAC and related products.\\r\\n• Serving niche markets across the United States.\\r\\n• Exposure includes high-growth data center and critical infrastructure segments.\\r\\n• Based in Orlando, Florida, with offices throughout the Midwest and Southwest.\\r\\n• Clavis partnered with the founding family in 2024 to expand the company’s presence and deepen sector focus.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nClavis portfolio source:\\r\\nhttps://claviscp.com/potfolio.html\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nKroll transaction note:\\r\\n• Clavis investment / partnership announced around December 2024.\\r\\n• Purpose included ambitious growth goals and operational excellence.\\r\\nhttps://dev-kroll.netlify.app/en/transactions/financial-advisor-atex\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCLAVIS — VERIFIED OPERATING PROFILE\\r\\nClavis publicly describes itself as:\\r\\n• “Experienced operators, not financial engineers.”\\r\\n• Focused on US-based manufacturing, industrial services, business services, infrastructure, aerospace & defense, distribution and supply chain.\\r\\n• Providing capital AND operational assistance.\\r\\n• Looking for businesses near an inflection point and ready for the next level.\\r\\n• Looking for strong leadership teams that want a partner, not just capital.\\r\\n• Seeking sustainable businesses with defensible growth opportunities.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nPublic investment criteria:\\r\\nhttps://www.claviscp.com/investment-focus.html\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nPublic homepage:\\r\\nhttps://www.claviscp.com/\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nIMPORTANT INTERPRETATION\\r\\nThis supports a useful thesis, but it is still a thesis:\\r\\nA-Tex is not simply receiving money from a passive financial sponsor. Clavis appears to operate with an industrial-operator mindset and to professionalize / scale companies after investment.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nThat makes the A-Tex Director concept coherent:\\r\\n• growth creates systems complexity.\\r\\n• acquisitions / expansion increase integration and governance needs.\\r\\n• a founder / CFO-led ERP model becomes harder to sustain.\\r\\n• internal ownership becomes more valuable as the business scales.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nDo NOT treat Clavis as the employer or assume Clavis personally sets Peter’s paycheck. A-Tex is the employer. Clavis can influence governance, budgeting, incentive structures, leadership hiring, and value-creation priorities, but the exact degree of control over this role is not publicly established.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nNETSUITE TEAM / ADMIN HIRING SIGNAL\\r\\nPublic-market research surfaced A-Tex NetSuite administration hiring activity.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nImportant discipline:\\r\\n• Do not assume the administrator reports to Peter.\\r\\n• Do not assume the administrator requisition was paused pending Peter.\\r\\n• Do not assume A-Tex changed the role from admin to Director because candidates refused relocation.\\r\\n• Those are plausible explanations, not verified facts.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nWhat the signal does tell us:\\r\\nA-Tex has recognized a need for dedicated NetSuite capability below the executive / Director ownership layer.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nThat creates a clean question for Friday:\\r\\n“How are you thinking about the internal systems team underneath this role over the next 6–12 months?”\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nIf John independently describes an admin / analyst / technical resource reporting into the role, that becomes strong confirmation that Peter is being hired to build and lead a function rather than merely serve as the senior administrator.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCOMPENSATION ARCHITECTURE — WORKING MODEL\\r\\nBase salary:\\r\\n• Target: $220K.\\r\\n• Floor: $200K.\\r\\n• Build family budget on guaranteed $200K, not variable compensation.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nAnnual bonus:\\r\\n• Do not assume a specific percentage until the plan is provided.\\r\\n• Working planning range for a Director-level role: approximately 10–20% target, subject to company practice.\\r\\n• At $200K:\\r\\n  - 10% target = $20K.\\r\\n  - 15% target = $30K.\\r\\n  - 20% target = $40K.\\r\\n• Target bonus is not guaranteed payout.\\r\\n• Need written answers on:\\r\\n  - target percentage.\\r\\n  - company vs individual weighting.\\r\\n  - thresholds.\\r\\n  - caps.\\r\\n  - discretionary vs formula-driven treatment.\\r\\n  - first-year proration.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n401(k):\\r\\n• Peter intends to max his own employee contribution.\\r\\n• Kayleen previously described a company contribution believed to be around 5%, but the exact formula and eligibility must be confirmed in writing.\\r\\n• Do not describe it as a match unless the plan says it is a match.\\r\\n• Could be non-elective employer contribution, match, profit-sharing contribution, or another qualified-plan contribution.\\r\\n• Vesting must be confirmed.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nManagement incentive / long-term incentive:\\r\\nUse this as the umbrella question:\\r\\n“Is there a management incentive or long-term incentive program for key leadership?”\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nPossible forms:\\r\\n• annual profit-based cash.\\r\\n• EBITDA / value-creation incentive.\\r\\n• management incentive pool.\\r\\n• phantom equity.\\r\\n• true equity.\\r\\n• transaction / liquidity incentive.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nDo not call retained earnings an employee benefit. Retained earnings are company equity/accounting value kept in the business. Employee economic participation would need to be delivered through an actual compensation or ownership mechanism.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nSix-month review:\\r\\nPeter has already told Tony he wants a six-month review.\\r\\nWorking logic:\\r\\n• $200K can be the starting floor while the role and scope are proven.\\r\\n• $220K is the six-month alignment target if the actual remit is the Director-level ownership being discussed.\\r\\n• This is not framed as a normal merit increase.\\r\\n• It is a scope / market alignment review based on demonstrated responsibilities, outcomes, and authority.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCOMPENSATION QUESTIONS FOR OFFER STAGE\\r\\n1. Exact base.\\r\\n2. Bonus target percentage.\\r\\n3. Bonus formula and weighting.\\r\\n4. First-year bonus proration.\\r\\n5. 401(k) employee and employer mechanics.\\r\\n6. Vesting.\\r\\n7. Medical / dental / vision cost.\\r\\n8. PTO / holidays.\\r\\n9. Travel / vacation stipend or card Peter recalls Kayleen discussing (approximately $1,600/year — must be verified).\\r\\n10. Relocation package.\\r\\n11. Any management incentive / LTI.\\r\\n12. Six-month review language.\\r\\n13. Title and reporting line.\\r\\n14. Budget / hiring authority.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nMARKET COMP SIGNAL\\r\\nCurrent Florida NetSuite-specific research found:\\r\\n• WatchGuard Technologies — Director of ERP Systems, Miami — posted approximately $195K–$205K base; NetSuite-centered internal leadership.\\r\\n• Riveron — Technology & Innovation Associate Director (NetSuite), Miami — posted approximately $120K–$210K, but client-facing consulting rather than the internal-owner model Peter wants.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nInterpretation:\\r\\nA $200K A-Tex floor is not contradicted by current Florida market evidence for senior NetSuite / ERP leadership.\\r\\nDo not claim these roles are exact comparables; use them as external market anchors.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCAREER VALUE OF A-TEX\\r\\nWhy this opportunity can be more valuable than another Principal Consultant role even before cash:\\r\\n• Internal ownership instead of multi-client churn.\\r\\n• One company, one roadmap, one leadership team.\\r\\n• Opportunity to create the systems function rather than inherit endless ticket execution.\\r\\n• Ability to build repeatable governance and institutional knowledge.\\r\\n• Direct connection between technology decisions and enterprise value.\\r\\n• Potential PE-backed transformation story.\\r\\n• Stronger future narrative for VP / CIO roles.\\r\\n• Better structural separation between work and home.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nMHI COMPARISON — CORE DISTINCTION\\r\\nBoth MHI and A-Tex would be full-time W-2 roles with benefits.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nMHI:\\r\\n• still consulting.\\r\\n• multiple clients.\\r\\n• billable / utilization economics.\\r\\n• different industries and project contexts.\\r\\n• go-lives and deadlines can bleed into home life.\\r\\n• success is largely client-delivery based.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nA-Tex:\\r\\n• internal leadership.\\r\\n• one enterprise.\\r\\n• one platform / ecosystem.\\r\\n• one roadmap.\\r\\n• deeper long-term accountability.\\r\\n• opportunity to build team, governance, and direction.\\r\\n• work can be more deliberately separated from home once operating rhythm stabilizes.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nThis is not a guarantee of a 5 PM job. It is a structural difference in the work model.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nFAMILY / OPERATING MODEL — CURRENT PLAN\\r\\nThis is family planning, NOT an agreed A-Tex employment arrangement.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nIf starting October 1:\\r\\n• Oct 16–19: first home visit target.\\r\\n• Nov 13–29: desired long home block, including a request to work remotely from Mexico during week of Nov 16 and remain home through Thanksgiving.\\r\\n• Dec 18–Jan 4: desired Christmas / New Year home block.\\r\\n• Months 4–6: test a repeatable rhythm based on actual role performance and company flexibility.\\r\\n• Six-month target: define a more durable on-site cadence, potentially concentrated Orlando weeks rather than constant weekend travel.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nDo not present Mexico remote work, PTO, or future once-a-month travel as agreed facts.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nFRIDAY WITH JOHN — INPUTS, NOT SCRIPT\\r\\nThe goal is to behave like the Director John would be handing the system to.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nPeter does NOT need to prove:\\r\\n• every NetSuite checkbox.\\r\\n• exact accounting-list configuration.\\r\\n• every serialized inventory transaction detail.\\r\\n• that he can personally execute every admin / architect / developer task.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nPeter DOES need to demonstrate:\\r\\n• he can understand current state quickly.\\r\\n• he can establish priorities.\\r\\n• he can separate process problems from system problems.\\r\\n• he can determine what belongs internally vs externally.\\r\\n• he can govern vendors and consultants.\\r\\n• he can connect Finance, Operations, Warehouse, Sales and IT.\\r\\n• he can build a roadmap.\\r\\n• he can create decision rights.\\r\\n• he can create a resource plan.\\r\\n• he can own outcomes.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nNatural Friday posture:\\r\\n“Show me the business and system you have today, where you are trying to take it, what is painful, what is already in flight, and where ownership currently breaks down.”\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nThen Peter can reason with John rather than perform a technical audition.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nFIRST 30 / 60 / 90 — WORKING DIRECTOR FRAME\\r\\nDo not over-engineer this before the next prep conversation.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nFirst 30:\\r\\n• listen.\\r\\n• map people, processes, systems, vendors, contracts and pain points.\\r\\n• stabilize obvious risk.\\r\\n• understand current NetSuite / RF-SMART / EDI / integration landscape.\\r\\n• identify quick wins and major dependencies.\\r\\n• establish executive / stakeholder cadence.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nDays 31–60:\\r\\n• publish prioritized roadmap.\\r\\n• separate operational fixes from structural initiatives.\\r\\n• define ownership and decision rights.\\r\\n• rationalize consultant / vendor usage.\\r\\n• begin standards, documentation and governance.\\r\\n• assess internal staffing needs.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nDays 61–90:\\r\\n• execute highest-value roadmap items.\\r\\n• quantify cost / risk / throughput improvements.\\r\\n• finalize resource plan.\\r\\n• establish recurring operating metrics.\\r\\n• create 6–12 month roadmap with leadership.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nThis should evolve after John explains actual current state.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nOPEN QUESTIONS / GATES\\r\\nThese are the things that still need evidence:\\r\\n• Final title.\\r\\n• Reporting line.\\r\\n• Exact scope across ERP vs broader IT.\\r\\n• Whether infrastructure / helpdesk / cybersecurity are included.\\r\\n• Current internal systems team.\\r\\n• Whether an admin / analyst hire is planned underneath the role.\\r\\n• Existing consultant roster and spend.\\r\\n• Current NetSuite pain points.\\r\\n• Active projects already committed.\\r\\n• M&A / expansion expectations.\\r\\n• Budget authority.\\r\\n• Vendor-selection authority.\\r\\n• Hiring authority.\\r\\n• Bonus architecture.\\r\\n• 401(k) mechanics.\\r\\n• LTI / management incentive.\\r\\n• Relocation package.\\r\\n• Initial on-site expectation.\\r\\n• Future remote flexibility.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nDISCONFIRMERS\\r\\nThe opportunity weakens materially if Friday / offer stage reveals:\\r\\n• Director title with no decision rights.\\r\\n• permanent one-man admin / helpdesk expectation.\\r\\n• little or no budget authority.\\r\\n• no resource path.\\r\\n• compensation stuck materially below $200K.\\r\\n• role is primarily configuration / ticket execution.\\r\\n• vague ownership while CFO retains all real decisions.\\r\\n• permanently unsustainable on-site expectations with no path to a workable family model.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCURRENT THESIS\\r\\nThe evidence currently supports continued pursuit.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nWhat has moved positively:\\r\\n• Director-level ownership language.\\r\\n• roadmap responsibility.\\r\\n• CFO relief / handoff theme.\\r\\n• systems-function-building possibility.\\r\\n• A-Tex growth / critical-infrastructure exposure.\\r\\n• Clavis operator-led investment model.\\r\\n• external Florida NetSuite Director compensation evidence near $200K.\\r\\n• family calendar can be structured around the transition if flexibility is ultimately agreed.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nWhat has NOT yet been proven:\\r\\n• exact authority.\\r\\n• exact compensation.\\r\\n• exact team structure.\\r\\n• exact long-term incentive.\\r\\n• exact remote cadence.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nNEXT GATE\\r\\nUse this artifact as the source package for the next prep conversation.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nNext work:\\r\\nBuild the Friday CFO interview attack plan from this evidence:\\r\\n1. opening posture.\\r\\n2. questions that transfer system ownership from John to Peter.\\r\\n3. current-state → future-state conversation flow.\\r\\n4. 30/60/90 discussion.\\r\\n5. team / admin / consultant structure.\\r\\n6. PE / growth / infrastructure context.\\r\\n7. what NOT to over-answer technically.\\r\\n8. close and next-step positioning.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nSTATUS: PROCEED\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nFRIDAY CFO WORKING-SESSION AGENDA — OUTBOUND VERSION\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nOBJECTIVE\\r\\nSet the tone before Friday so John can enter the meeting knowing this will be a structured working session about current state, priorities, ownership, roadmap, and operating model — not an unstructured technical interview.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nOUTBOUND EMAIL DRAFT\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nSubject: Friday — working session outline\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nJohn —\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nLooking forward to Friday. Since we have 90 minutes, I wanted to put a little structure around the conversation so we can make the best use of the time and keep it focused on the areas that matter most.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nBased on our prior conversations, I thought the discussion could center on five things:\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n1. Where the business and systems environment sit today — NetSuite, RF-SMART, EDI, integrations, outside partners, and anything already in flight.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Where you see the biggest pain points, risks, and priorities over the next 6–12 months.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n3. How ownership is structured today across Finance, Operations, Warehouse, Sales, outside consultants, and internal resources — and where you would most like that ownership to become cleaner.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n4. How I would think about the roadmap: immediate stabilization / quick wins, structural improvements, internal versus external resources, and the sequence for the first 90 days.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n5. What success looks like for the role from your perspective — both in the near term and as the company continues to grow.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nNo need to prepare anything formal. I’d rather use the time as a working conversation around what you are actually dealing with today and where you want the function to go.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nLooking forward to it.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nPeter\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nWHY THIS VERSION WORKS\\r\\n• It reflects topics already discussed with John rather than introducing a new presentation.\\r\\n• It gives John advance notice without creating homework.\\r\\n• It positions Peter at Director altitude: current state, ownership, priorities, roadmap, resourcing, outcomes.\\r\\n• It naturally invites John to bring current problems, active initiatives, and leadership context into the meeting.\\r\\n• It creates room for John to discuss whatever comes out of his upper-management meetings before Friday.\\r\\n• It avoids compensation, relocation, title negotiation, and the speculative admin-hiring theory.\\r\\n• It does not overcommit Peter to a prebuilt solution before he sees the actual current state.\\r\\n• It reduces the probability of the meeting collapsing into isolated NetSuite trivia.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nINTERNAL 90-MINUTE FLOW — NOT FOR EMAIL\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n0–10 minutes — Business / leadership context\\r\\n• What changed since the last conversation?\\r\\n• What came out of current leadership discussions?\\r\\n• What is creating urgency around this role now?\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n10–30 minutes — Current state\\r\\n• NetSuite environment.\\r\\n• RF-SMART / warehouse.\\r\\n• EDI.\\r\\n• integrations.\\r\\n• reporting / finance.\\r\\n• outside consultants / vendors.\\r\\n• known pain points.\\r\\n• projects already underway.\\r\\n• ownership gaps.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n30–50 minutes — Future state / priorities\\r\\n• What does John want off his plate?\\r\\n• What does leadership want different in 6–12 months?\\r\\n• What is operationally painful versus strategically limiting?\\r\\n• Which initiatives matter because of growth, scale, acquisitions, or infrastructure demands?\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n50–70 minutes — Peter’s operating model\\r\\n• process first, system second.\\r\\n• stabilize before redesign.\\r\\n• roadmap by business value / risk / dependency.\\r\\n• internal capability versus consulting support.\\r\\n• governance / decision rights.\\r\\n• team / resource architecture.\\r\\n• first 30 / 60 / 90 days.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n70–85 minutes — Role definition\\r\\n• decision authority.\\r\\n• reporting line.\\r\\n• internal team.\\r\\n• consultant/vendor ownership.\\r\\n• budget/resource expectations.\\r\\n• broader IT / enterprise systems boundary.\\r\\n• success measures.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n85–90 minutes — Close\\r\\n• summarize what Peter heard.\\r\\n• confirm where the role creates the most leverage.\\r\\n• identify remaining diligence / next step.\\r\\n• keep commercial terms with Tony unless John explicitly raises them.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nGUARDRAIL\\r\\nThe agenda is intended to create structure, not make John feel managed. Keep the email short, conversational, and collaborative. Do not attach a deck or detailed 30/60/90 before Friday unless John asks for one.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n2026-09-21 — JOHN FRIDAY PREP ANCHOR COMMIT\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nGUIDING OPERATING PRINCIPLE\\r\\nThe first 90 days are not the transformation. They are the runway that lets Peter assume ownership without breaking the business.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n30 DAYS — ONBOARD / GROUND\\r\\n• Learn the business before changing it.\\r\\n• Understand the people, departments, management team, warehouse, finance, sales, operations, vendors, consultants, IT support, NetSuite, RF-SMART, EDI, integrations, reporting, item/master-data structure, active projects, and known pain points.\\r\\n• Get introduced early to the consulting group, IT group, major vendors, and any other external partners.\\r\\n• Build the relationships immediately even if the associated work will not be tackled until day 30, 60, or later.\\r\\n• Meet department leaders and ask each for their 5–10 biggest systems/process problems.\\r\\n• Capture pain points without promising that every request becomes a project.\\r\\n• Build the real current-state map and validate the existing roadmap.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n60 DAYS — STABLE / QUICK WINS\\r\\n• Peter understands the NetSuite environment and operating context well enough to act with confidence.\\r\\n• Prioritize the department pain-point inventory by business impact, urgency, dependency, effort, and correct owner.\\r\\n• Attack high-impact / low-effort quick wins.\\r\\n• Prototype small fixes when appropriate.\\r\\n• Separate:\\r\\n  - work Peter should do personally.\\r\\n  - work that should be delegated internally.\\r\\n  - work that belongs with a consultant/vendor.\\r\\n  - work that should be deferred into the roadmap.\\r\\n• Establish a standing executive priority review with John or the appropriate sponsor.\\r\\n• Establish delivery / follow-up cadence with department owners.\\r\\n• Start taking recurring system ownership off John and other leaders.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n90 DAYS — ROADMAP EXECUTION\\r\\n• Peter is no longer onboarding.\\r\\n• Begin executing the larger roadmap.\\r\\n• Rationalize outside partners and vendors.\\r\\n• Review item/master-data/process architecture where it materially affects the roadmap.\\r\\n• Begin structural integration / EDI / RF-SMART / reporting / governance work.\\r\\n• Define the internal resource model and team needs.\\r\\n• Quantify early business wins and recurring pain removed.\\r\\n• Build the 6–12 month execution roadmap.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n90+ DAYS — REAL DIRECTOR PHASE\\r\\n• This is where the larger transformation begins.\\r\\n• Own prioritization, architecture direction, resource allocation, vendor strategy, governance, and executive communication.\\r\\n• Build internal capability where it creates leverage.\\r\\n• Use consultants as targeted capacity/specialists, not as owners of A-Tex institutional knowledge.\\r\\n• Maintain one coherent systems roadmap tied to business growth.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nDAY-ONE / WEEK-ONE MANAGEMENT MOVE\\r\\nRequest a short meeting with the management/director team.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nAsk each department:\\r\\n“Give me the 5–10 systems or process problems that frustrate you most, slow your team down, create risk, or stop you from getting information you need.”\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nThe point is not to create a 100-ticket backlog.\\r\\nThe point is to build a cross-functional pain inventory and identify:\\r\\n• immediate wins.\\r\\n• recurring root causes.\\r\\n• process problems masquerading as system problems.\\r\\n• technical debt.\\r\\n• reporting gaps.\\r\\n• ownership gaps.\\r\\n• vendor/consultant failures.\\r\\n• roadmap-level issues.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nPRIORITIZATION MODEL\\r\\nEvery request gets screened for:\\r\\n• business impact.\\r\\n• urgency.\\r\\n• dependency.\\r\\n• effort.\\r\\n• risk.\\r\\n• correct owner.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nPossible outcomes:\\r\\n• Peter solves it now.\\r\\n• Peter proves a concept quickly.\\r\\n• Internal resource owns it.\\r\\n• Consultant/vendor owns it under Peter’s direction.\\r\\n• Roadmap item.\\r\\n• Not worth doing / deprioritize.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nEXECUTIVE CADENCE\\r\\nTwo distinct rhythms:\\r\\n1. PRIORITY REVIEW\\r\\n   • Peter + John / executive sponsor.\\r\\n   • Agree what matters most.\\r\\n   • Reconcile department pressure against enterprise priorities.\\r\\n   • Confirm decisions / resources / escalations.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n2. DELIVERY REVIEW\\r\\n   • Peter + relevant department owners / resources.\\r\\n   • Check progress.\\r\\n   • Unblock decisions.\\r\\n   • Hold internal/external owners accountable.\\r\\n   • Close the loop so requests do not disappear.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCONSULTANT / VENDOR POST-MORTEM\\r\\nOne of the first important discovery questions:\\r\\n“If you have been through multiple consultants or partners, what happened?”\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nUnderstand:\\r\\n• why prior partners were hired.\\r\\n• where they succeeded.\\r\\n• where they failed.\\r\\n• scope problems.\\r\\n• architecture problems.\\r\\n• project-management problems.\\r\\n• turnover / continuity problems.\\r\\n• knowledge-transfer failures.\\r\\n• expectation gaps.\\r\\n• whether A-Tex lacked a strong internal owner.\\r\\n• which partners should stay.\\r\\n• which relationships should be changed or ended.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCORE THESIS\\r\\nIf A-Tex has historically moved from consultant to consultant, Peter becomes the continuity layer.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nConsultants do not own the company’s systems knowledge.\\r\\nA-Tex owns the knowledge.\\r\\nPeter owns the roadmap and directs outside expertise against bounded work.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nINTRODUCTIONS ARE A FIRST-30-DAY REQUIREMENT\\r\\nPeter should explicitly ask John:\\r\\n• Introduce me to the consulting group.\\r\\n• Introduce me to the IT group.\\r\\n• Introduce me to the key software / integration vendors.\\r\\n• Introduce me to the department leaders whose work depends most on the systems environment.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nThe objective is relationship creation, not immediate intervention.\\r\\nA problem can wait 60 days.\\r\\nThe relationship should not.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nFRIDAY JOHN WORKING SESSION — KEY CONFIRMATION TARGETS\\r\\nUse Friday to understand and validate:\\r\\n• what John needs off his plate.\\r\\n• where systems ownership currently breaks.\\r\\n• who owns what today.\\r\\n• which departments feel the most pain.\\r\\n• what external partners are doing now.\\r\\n• consultant history and failure modes.\\r\\n• where the business is going over the next 6–12 months.\\r\\n• what Clavis / ownership / executive leadership is prioritizing.\\r\\n• whether data-center / critical-infrastructure growth is central, emerging, or only one segment.\\r\\n• what role Peter is actually being hired to own.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nDo not force the hypothesis.\\r\\nAsk questions that allow John to confirm, correct, or reject it.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nFRIDAY POSTURE\\r\\nPeter is not selling a completed solution.\\r\\nPeter is demonstrating how he assumes ownership:\\r\\nlisten → map → prioritize → prove quick wins → establish governance → execute roadmap → build the function.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nSTATUS: PROCEED\\r\\nCONFIDENCE: 96%\\r\\nNEXT GATE: Build the dedicated Friday handoff / working-session prompt from this anchor and remove A-Tex tactical prep from the overloaded general job-search summary.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n2026-09-22 — COMMERCIAL / RELOCATION / FRIDAY DELTA\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nTONY COMMERCIAL SIGNAL\\r\\nPeter’s commercial framework to recruiter Tony LaBella:\\r\\n• $220K target base.\\r\\n• $200K floor.\\r\\n• relocation support.\\r\\n• Director-level scope / authority.\\r\\n• formal six-month review of scope, compensation, and operating structure.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nTony indicated he believes alignment is possible on the points raised and will provide further feedback before the next call.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nInterpretation:\\r\\n• Strong positive recruiter-level signal.\\r\\n• Not an approved offer.\\r\\n• Do not walk the $200K floor backward without new information.\\r\\n• Let Friday with John validate Director-level scope, then close commercial terms through Tony.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nRELOCATION WORKING STRUCTURE\\r\\n• Peter is currently in Las Vegas after transitioning out of Los Angeles.\\r\\n• Initial move is Peter only, not full-family relocation.\\r\\n• Preferred company support: direct-booked travel plus furnished temporary housing / Airbnb near the office.\\r\\n• Current ask: temporary housing through approximately Nov 1.\\r\\n• Peter will handle longer-term housing after establishing himself on the ground.\\r\\n• Peter is prepared to be on-site for the initial six-month period if package and mandate align.\\r\\n• If A-Tex wants an Orlando visit immediately after Friday to meet ownership / leadership, Peter can move quickly.\\r\\n• Working start target if the process closes: Oct 6–12.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nFRIDAY OWNERSHIP PRINCIPLE\\r\\nJohn’s role: sponsor the handoff and make the introductions.\\r\\nPeter’s role: own the relationships and work.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCritical early introductions:\\r\\n• consulting group.\\r\\n• IT group.\\r\\n• key vendors / integration partners.\\r\\n• department leaders.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nThe relationship should start immediately even if the associated work waits until day 30, 60, or later.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nCore model:\\r\\nJohn sponsors → Peter owns relationship → Peter owns prioritization → Peter owns follow-through → John re-enters only when CFO authority is required.\"}]},\"error\":null,\"error_data\":null,\"error_code\":null,\"classification_id\":null,\"json_rpc_error_code\":null,\"error_http_status_code\":null,\"error_http_headers\":null,\"internal\":{\"external_call_time_ms\":1185},\"clamp_errors\":null,\"clamp_rewrites\":null,\"connector_id\":\"connector_5f3c8c41a1e54ad7a76272c89e2554fa\",\"connector_name\":\"Google Drive\",\"action_name\":\"search\",\"is_sync\":false}"}, "create_time": 1790199317.6615129, "end_turn": null, "